<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE chapter SYSTEM "http://phoenix.int.thomsonreuters.com/dtd/pax.dtd">
<chapter date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
    <metadata field="right.running.head">
      <value>Saskatchewan Climate Change Law and Policy</value>
    </metadata>
  </metadata.block>
  <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart releaseDate="2022;10;30" sp=";;1" folio="7-1" releaseNumber="3" hyphenated="n" nep=";;5" backBlank="n" backFolio="7-2"?>
  <front date.updated="0">
    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
      <label date.updated="0">Chapter</label>
      <designator date.updated="0">7</designator>
      <name date.updated="0">Saskatchewan Climate Change Law and Policy<footnote date.updated="0">
          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">&ast;</footnote.reference>
          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">John D. Stefaniuk is a Partner in Thompson Dorfman Sweatman LLP. Patricia Farnese is an Associate Professor of Law at the University of Saskatchewan.</para.text>
            </para>
          </footnote.body>
        </footnote>
      </name>
    </outline.name.block>
  </front>
  <chapter.body date.updated="0">
    <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
      <front date.updated="0">
        <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
          <designator date.updated="0">I</designator>
          <name date.updated="0">Overview</name>
        </outline.name.block>
      </front>
      <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
        <section.block>
          <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <section.front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                <designator date.updated="0">7:1</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Generally</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </section.front>
            <section.body date.updated="0">
              <para date.updated="20221004">
                <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan occupies a fairly unique position among Canadian provinces in the context of climate change. It has experienced remarkable growth in greenhouse gas (&ldquo;GHG&rdquo;) emissions over the last decade, fueled by a corresponding growth in natural resource production, especially growth in the petroleum industry. Although most of this energy production has been for export rather than domestic consumption, the effect has been skyrocketing per capita GHG emissions in the province. In response, in 2007, the government of the day unveiled an ambitious program to reduce Saskatchewan&apos;s per capita GHG emissions, in the form of the <ital>Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007.</ital>
                  <footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="20221004">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                            <ref ref.id="I0a0d5c10496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan, Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan 2007</ref>
                        </cite>.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> However, with a subsequent change of government, virtually all references to that plan vanished from government policy and program documents, and the new government determined to chart a different course in the form of Bill 95, <ital>The Management and Reduction of Greenhouse Gases Act</ital> (<ital>MRGGA</ital>).<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">2nd Sess., 26th Leg., Saskatchewan, 2009.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Bill 95 was subsequently revised and reintroduced.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">3rd Sess., 26th Leg., Saskatchewan, 2009.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> The new <ital>MRGGA</ital> received Royal Assent on May 3, 2010, but was not proclaimed until 2018 after substantial amendments were made. Together with <ital>The Environmental Management and Protection Act, 2010</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">S.S. 2010, c. E-10.22. Assented to May 20, 2010 and proclaimed in force June 1, 2015.</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> and its companion, <ital>The Saskatchewan Environmental Code</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">The Code is available at <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                            <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0a0e9490496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6">http://publications.saskatchewan.ca/&num;/categories/69</ref>
                        </cite> (accessed October 2019).</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> the <ital>MRGGA</ital> implements Saskatchewan&apos;s current climate plan. Known as <ital>Prairie Resilience: A Made-in-Saskatchewan Climate Strategy</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Saskatchewan, Prairie Resilience: A Made-In-Saskatchewan Climate Change Strategy (December 2017), available at <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                            <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief2cef6022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/business/environmental-protection-and-sustainability/a-made-in-saskatchewan-climate-change-strategy/saskatchewans-climate-change-strategy</ref>
                        </cite> (accessed October 2019).</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> this plan builds on the government&apos;s 2016 White Paper on Climate Change where the role of technology and innovation as a means of addressing climate change was featured prominently.<footnote date.updated="0">
                    <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                    <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">
                          <trace>
                            <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                              <ref ref.id="I0a0e9493496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Government of Saskatchewan, Climate Change White Paper (October 2016)</ref>
                            </cite>
                          </trace>, available at <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                            <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0a0ebba0496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6">https://www.scribd.com/document/328041639/Saskatchewan-White-Paper-on-Climte-Change&num;from_embed</ref>
                        </cite> (accessed October 2019).</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </footnote.body>
                </footnote> Furthermore, Saskatchewan has rejected the use of a carbon price as a means to reduce emissions.</para.text>
              </para>
            </section.body>
          </section>
        </section.block>
      </analytical.level.body>
    </analytical.level>
    <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
      <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
        <metadata field="classification">
          <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
        </metadata>
      </metadata.block>
      <front date.updated="0">
        <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
          <designator date.updated="0">II</designator>
          <name date.updated="0">Saskatchewan&apos;s Climate Change Profile</name>
        </outline.name.block>
      </front>
      <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
        <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
          <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
            <metadata field="classification">
              <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
            </metadata>
          </metadata.block>
          <front date.updated="0">
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
              <designator date.updated="0">A</designator>
              <name date.updated="0">Greenhouse Gas Emissions</name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </front>
          <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
            <section.block>
              <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                  <metadata field="classification">
                    <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
                  </metadata>
                </metadata.block>
                <section.front date.updated="0">
                  <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                    <designator date.updated="0">7:2</designator>
                    <name date.updated="0">Historical Emissions</name>
                  </outline.name.block>
                </section.front>
                <section.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">In 202<trace>4</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/>, Saskatchewan generated <trace>9</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/>&percnt; of Canada&apos;s total GHG emissions, or <trace>7 2</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/> Megatonnes, down from <trace>83</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/> Mt in 2005 <trace.deleted/>
                      <trace>In  2023, </trace>
                      <trace.deleted/>
                      <trace>p</trace>er capita emissions from Saskatchewan <trace.deleted/>decreased from 71.6 to <trace>61.07</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/> tonnes of GHGs per perso<trace>n</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/> as a result of Saskatchewan&apos;s declining emission rates as well as its growing <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;5" nep=";;7" hyphenated="y" folio="7-5" backFolio="7-6" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>population<trace.deleted/>
                      <footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Canadian Centre for Energy Information. &ldquo;Greenhouse Gas Emissions &ndash; National Inventory Report (NIR),&rdquo; (last modified 13 May 2025), online (website): <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                                <ref normalpub="webref">https://energy-information.canada.ca/en/subjects/greenhouse-gas-emissions-national-inventory-report-nir</ref>
                              </cite>
                            </para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>. This is roughly <trace.deleted/>
                      <trace>3.4</trace> times the GHG emissions generated by its neighbour, Manitoba, in absolute terms and almost <trace>four</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/> times on a per capita basis.<trace>2</trace>
                      <footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                            </para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote> This comparison is of some interest, since both provinces have a similar continental climate and a similar population. Saskatchewan&apos;s GHG emissions have increased from 4<trace>8</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/> Mega tonnes to <trace>7 2</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/> Mt from 1990 to 202<trace>4</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/> From 2005 to 202<trace>4</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/>, Saskatchewan <trace.deleted/>experienced a <trace>13</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/>&percnt; decrease of GHG emissions.<trace.deleted/> It is interesting to observe that until recently it seemed as though Saskatchewan&apos;s GHG emissions would increase rather than decrease. In 201<trace>5</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/>, Saskatchewan saw its highest recorded emissions in recent history with 8<trace>9</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/> Mt of emissions. The province then saw a slight decrease to <trace>87</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/> Mt in 20<trace>19</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/>, and then a dramatic drop to <trace>72</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/> Mt in 202<trace>4</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/>. Saskatchewan was not alone in this regard. Ontario and <trace.deleted/>
                      <trace>Alberta</trace> also saw significant drops in their GHG emissions.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <trace.deleted/>
                              <trace>Government of Canada, &ldquo;National Inventory Report 1990&ndash;2024: Greenhouse Gas Sources and Sinks in Canada &ndash; Executive Summary 2026.&rdquo; (last modified 2026), online: https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/climate-change/greenhouse-gas-emissions/sources-sinks-executive-summary-2026.html&num;toc5</trace>
                            </para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </para>
                  <para date.updated="20221004">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan produces approximately one-third of the world&apos;s potash and uranium. Saskatchewan also produces approximately one-third of Canada&apos;s oil production and is second only to Alberta. In 20 years, oil and gas production has more than doubled in Saskatchewan. Saskatchewan is also a significant coal producer, ranking behind only Alberta and British Columbia.</para.text>
                  </para>
                  <para date.updated="20221004">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">The oil and gas sector is the single largest contributor to Saskatchewan&apos;s total GHG emissions. As of 20<trace>23</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/>, oil and gas was responsible for <trace>roughly </trace>3<trace>5</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/>&percnt; of all provincial emissions. It is followed by the agriculture sector with 2<trace>2</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/>&percnt;. Enteric fermentation associated with livestock production is a significant source of agriculture&apos;s emissions. Increases in agriculture&apos;s emissions in recent years can also be contribute<trace>d</trace> to increased use of synthetic nitrogen fertilizer. After agriculture, electricity generation produces 19&percnt; and transportation 1<trace>2</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/>
                    </para.text>
                  </para>
                  <para date.updated="20221004">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan&apos;s continental climate, with warm summers and very cold winters, generates demand for heat and cooling. In Saskatchewan, electric generation is also a major source of GHG emissions.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Ministry of the Environment, &ldquo;Saskatchewan Environmental Code: Moving Forward in Partnership&rdquo; (November 2014), available at <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief2e4ef022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">http://mcmillan.ca/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/Default.aspx_.pdf</ref>
                            </cite>.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote> SaskPower is Saskatchewan&apos;s Crown-owned electric power utility. Coal-fired power plants generate approximately 37&percnt; of Saskatchewan&apos;s electricity, with natural gas-fired plants representing another 43&percnt; of supply.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="20221004">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                <ref ref.id="I0a0f09c3496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">SaskPower, Powering Saskatchewan to a cleaner Energy Future: 2016-2017 Annual Report, at 10</ref>
                              </cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief2e9d1022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.saskpower.com/-/media/SaskPower/About-Us/Reports/Past-Reports/Report-AnnualReport-2019-20.ashx</ref>
                            </cite> (accessed August 2017).</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote> The remaining demand for electricity is supplied through hydroelectric development (approximately 15&percnt;) and wind power (approximately 3&percnt;), and other sources (approximately 1&percnt;). Of SaskPower&apos;s 2019-20 generating capacity of 4,893 MW, 4,121 MW was supplied from SaskPower&apos;s facilities and the balance of 772 through long-term power-purchase agreements.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">SaskPower, Powering Saskatchewan to a cleaner Energy Future: 2016-2017 Annual Report, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief2eeb3022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.saskpower.com/-/media/SaskPower/About-Us/Reports/Past-Reports/Report-AnnualReport-2019-20.ashx</ref>
                            </cite> (accessed August 2017).</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </para>
                </section.body>
              </section>
              <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                  <metadata field="classification">
                    <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
                  </metadata>
                </metadata.block>
                <section.front date.updated="0">
                  <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                    <designator date.updated="0">7:3</designator>
                    <name date.updated="0">Projected Emissions</name>
                  </outline.name.block>
                </section.front>
                <section.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan&apos;s per capita emissions of <trace>36.97</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/> tonnes carbon dioxide equivalent are <trace>10.25</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/>&percnt; of Canada&apos;s <trace.deleted/> That amounts to <trace.deleted/>
                      <trace>However, between 2005 and 2024, GHG emissions in Saskatchewan were reduced by 13&percnt;, which is higher than the National average. Additionally, emissions decreased by 10.9 Mt CO2 eq, primarily due to emission reductions from the oil and gas sector. Over the first year of the pandemic, from 2019 to 2020, a reduction in GHG emissions was observed for all provinces and territories. Emission levels in 2024 were below the pre-pandemic levels observed in 2019 for most provinces and territories, except Yukon and Nunavut.</trace>
                      <trace.deleted/>
                    </para.text>
                  </para>
                  <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">It was previously predicted that Saskatchewan&apos;s GHG emissions were not expected to appreciably reduce until 2030. As previously noted,<trace.deleted/>
                      <trace> emissions have been steadily declining since 2021, with Saskatchewan&apos;s lowest production total being recorded in 2024.</trace> Only more recent data will reveal whether or not that reduction was temporary or the beginning of a real trend.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Environment and Climate Change Canada, Canada&apos;s Second Biennial Report on Climate Change, at 38, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief2f606022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/climate-change/greenhouse-gas-emissions/second-biennial-report.html</ref>
                            </cite>.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </para>
                </section.body>
              </section>
            </section.block>
          </analytical.level.body>
        </analytical.level>
        <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
          <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
            <metadata field="classification">
              <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
            </metadata>
          </metadata.block>
          <front date.updated="0">
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
              <designator date.updated="0">B</designator>
              <name date.updated="0">Climate Change Impacts</name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </front>
          <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
            <section.block>
              <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                  <metadata field="classification">
                    <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
                  </metadata>
                </metadata.block>
                <section.front date.updated="0">
                  <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                    <designator date.updated="0">7:4</designator>
                    <name date.updated="0">Historical Impacts</name>
                  </outline.name.block>
                </section.front>
                <section.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">The Canadian Prairie Provinces have seen a warming trend over the last 100 years, with yearly average temperatures increasing approximately 1.5&deg; Celsius&mdash;winter temperatures warming by 3.1&deg;C, and summer temperatures by 1.5&deg;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                <ref ref.id="I0a0f57e3496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Environment and Climate Change Canada, Climate data and scenarios for Canada: Synthesis of recent observations and modelling results (2016), at 5</ref>
                              </cite>, available at <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                                <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0a0f57e4496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6">https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/climate-change/publications/data-scenarios-synthesis-recent-observation.html</ref>
                            </cite> (accessed March 2020).</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote> The date of the last spring frost occurs about 10 days earlier on average, and the length of the growing season on the Prairies has increased by some 10 to 15 days when compared to the 1940s and early 1950s. There have been fewer days of extreme cold in the winter, snow cover has decreased and spring run-off begins earlier than in past years.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                <ref ref.id="I0a0f57e5496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Natural Resources Canada, The Winds of Change: Climate Change in Prairie Provinces (September 12, 2007)</ref>
                              </cite>, available at <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                                <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0a0f7ef0496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6">http://publications.gc.ca/site/eng/96166/publication.html</ref>
                              </cite> (accessed March 2020), citing <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                <ref ref.id="I0a0f7ef1496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">H.W. Cutforth, B.G. McConkey, R.J. Woodvine, D.G. Smith, P.G. Jefferson and O.O. Akinremi, &ldquo;Climate Change in the Semiarid Prairie of Southwestern Saskatchewan&rdquo;, Canadian Journal of Plant Science</ref>
                              </cite> (upcoming issue); <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                <ref ref.id="I0a0f7ef2496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">H.G. Hengeveld, &ldquo;Projections for Canada&apos;s Climate Future: A Discussion of Recent Simulations with the Canadian Global Climate Model&rdquo;, Climate Change Digest 00-01 (Ottawa: Environment Canada, 2000)</ref>
                              </cite>; <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                <ref ref.id="I0a0f7ef3496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">G.D.V. Williams, R.A. Fautley, K.H. Jones, R.B. Stewart and E.E. Wheaton, &ldquo;Estimating Effects of Climate Change on Agriculture in Saskatachewan, Canada&rdquo; in The Impact of Climate Variations on Agriculture: Assessments in Cool Temperature and Cold Regions, Vol. 1, M.L. Parry et al., eds. (Boston: Kluwer Academic Publishers, 1988), at pp. 221-382</ref>
                            </cite>.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote> Within the last hundred years, the Prairies have experienced two periods with higher than normal temperatures and lower precipitation: the dust bowl years of the 1930s, where rainfall was only 60&percnt; of normal, and the even more severe drought of 1988, where one-half the normal precipitation was met with higher than average temperatures during the growing season.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="20221004">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                <ref ref.id="I0a0f7ef4496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">International Institute for Sustainable Development and the Environmental Adaptation Research Group, Agriculture and Climate Change&mdash;A Prairie Perspective (Institute for Environmental Studies, University of Toronto, 1997)</ref>
                              </cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3098e022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.iisd.org/system/files/publications/agriculture_climate.pdf</ref>
                            </cite> (accessed March 2020).</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </para>
                </section.body>
              </section>
              <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                  <metadata field="classification">
                    <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
                  </metadata>
                </metadata.block>
                <section.front date.updated="0">
                  <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                    <designator date.updated="0">7:5</designator>
                    <name date.updated="0">Projected Impacts</name>
                  </outline.name.block>
                </section.front>
                <section.body date.updated="0">
                  <para date.updated="20221004">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">The main effect of climate change in Saskatchewan will likely be on the supply of water. Saskatchewan lacks the moderating effect of large bodies of water. Changes in temperature in Saskatchewan are expected to be greater than in those locations located near large bodies of water. This is expected to result in increased evaporation during the warmer summer months without corresponding increases in precipitation. This could result in increases in the frequency and duration of drought conditions in the province. Where water is available, higher temperatures may result in longer growing seasons, particularly in the northern parts of Saskatchewan. Soil moisture deficits are expected to increase.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                <ref ref.id="I0a0fa601496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Elaine M. Barrow and Dave Sauchyn, &ldquo;Uncertainty in climate projections and time of emergence of climate signals in the western Canadian Prairies&rdquo;, International Journal of Climatology (March 2019), DOI: 10.1002/joc.6079</ref>
                            </cite>.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </para>
                  <para date.updated="20221004">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">The timing of precipitation is expected to change, with more winter precipitation, greater supplies of water from spring run-offs and drier summers. Aquatic habitats are expected to be stressed and various fish species and some waterfowl populations are expected to decline substantially. Warmer winters will result in reduced storage of water as ice and snow over the winter and reduced ability to rely upon winter roads as winter transportation to inaccessible areas. Warmer winters will also allow non-native plants and animals to establish themselves, including insect pests, such as the mountain pine beetle, which would otherwise not survive the cold winters.<footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">
                              <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                <ref ref.id="I0a0fa602496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">D. Sauchyn and S. Kulshreshtha, &ldquo;Prairies&rdquo;, in From Impacts to Adaptation: Canada in a Changing Climate 2007, D.S. Lemmen, F.J. Warren, J. Lacrox and E. Bush, eds. (Ottawa: Natural Resources Canada, 2008)</ref>
                              </cite>, available at <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                                <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0a0fa603496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6">https://www.nrcan.gc.ca/environment/resources/publications/impacts-adaptation/reports/assessments/2008/10253</ref>
                            </cite> (accessed March 2020).</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </para.text>
                  </para>
                </section.body>
              </section>
            </section.block>
          </analytical.level.body>
        </analytical.level>
      </analytical.level.body>
    </analytical.level>
    <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;9" nep=";;11" hyphenated="n" folio="7-9" backFolio="7-10" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>
    <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
      <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
        <metadata field="classification">
          <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
        </metadata>
      </metadata.block>
      <front date.updated="0">
        <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
          <designator date.updated="0">III</designator>
          <name date.updated="0">Legal Framework</name>
        </outline.name.block>
      </front>
      <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
        <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
          <front date.updated="0">
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
              <designator date.updated="0">A</designator>
              <name date.updated="0">Constitutional Framework</name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </front>
          <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
            <section.block>
            </trace.deleted>
          </section.block>
        </analytical.level.body>
      </analytical.level>
      <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
        <front date.updated="0">
          <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
            <designator date.updated="0">B</designator>
            <name date.updated="0">Legislative and Regulatory Framework</name>
          </outline.name.block>
        </front>
        <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
          <section.block>
            <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
              <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                <metadata field="classification">
                  <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
                </metadata>
              </metadata.block>
              <section.front date.updated="0">
                <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                  <designator date.updated="0">7:7</designator>
                  <name date.updated="0">Introduction</name>
                </outline.name.block>
              </section.front>
              <section.body date.updated="0">
                <para date.updated="20221004">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">In response to the federal <ital>Greenhouse Gas Pollution Pricing Act</ital>
                    <footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">S.C. 2018, c. 12.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote> that imposes a carbon tax on jurisdictions that fail to implement a pollution pricing system, Saskatchewan released <ital>Prairie Resilience: A Made-in-Saskatchewan Climate Change Strategy.</ital> Under <ital>Prairie Resilience</ital>, <ital>The Management and Reduction of Greenhouse Gases Act</ital>
                    <footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">S.S. 2010, c. M-2.01, as amended by the <ital>Statutes of Saskatchewan</ital>, 2013, c.13; 2014, c.E-13.1; and 2018, c.48.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> (&ldquo;<ital>MRGGA</ital>&rdquo;) was finally proclaimed after being first introduced in 2009. The <ital>MRGGA</ital>, combined with other environmental regulations, constitute the legislative and regulatory framework for climate change mitigation and adaptation in Saskatchewan.</para.text>
                </para>
              </section.body>
            </section>
            <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
              <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                <metadata field="classification">
                  <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
                  <value>ENV.III.1.g Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Miscellaneous</value>
                </metadata>
              </metadata.block>
              <section.front date.updated="0">
                <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                  <designator date.updated="0">7:8</designator>
                  <name date.updated="0">Environment<sep/>General Legislation</name>
                </outline.name.block>
              </section.front>
              <section.body date.updated="0">
                <head align="l" toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <name date.updated="0">
                      <bold>
                        <ital>The Environmental Management and Protection Act, 2010</ital>
                      </bold>
                    </name>
                  </name.block>
                </head>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">
                    <ital>The Environmental Management and Protection Act, 2010</ital>
                    <footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">S.S. 2010, c. E-10.22, as amended by the <ital>Statutes of Saskatchewan</ital>, 2013, c.20, c.27 and c.32; 2014, c.E-13.1; and 2018, c.9 and c.10.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> is Saskatchewan&apos;s omnibus environmental protection and management legislation. This Act was proclaimed on June 1, 2015, repealing <ital>The Clean Air Act</ital>, <ital>The Environmental Management and Protection Act, 2002</ital>, <ital>The Litter Control Act</ital>, and <ital>The State of the Environment Report Act</ital>, and making consequential amendments to certain other Acts. Upon proclamation, <ital>The Environmental Management and Protection Act, 2010</ital> introduced the <ital>Saskatchewan Environment Code</ital> and its supporting regulations.</para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">Part III of this Act establishes a prohibition against unauthorized discharges<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Section 2(1)(g): &ldquo;&lsquo;Discharge&rsquo; means discharge, drainage, deposit, release or emission into the environment.&rdquo;</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> and pollution. Section 8 creates a prohibition against discharging or allowing the discharge of a substance into the environment in an amount, concentration or level or at a rate of release that may cause or is causing an adverse effect unless otherwise expressly authorized by legislation or approval therein. Section 9(1) then creates a duty upon any person who creates or permits an unauthorized discharge to report such discharge.</para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">This Act also imposes a duty to report the discovery of substances in specific situations. Section 9(2) imposes a duty upon any person who owns or occupies land on which a substance is discovered that may cause or is causing an adverse effect. Section 9(3) imposes a duty to report upon any person who, while conducting work, discovers a substance that may cause or is causing an adverse effect. There is a further duty to take immediate remedial action in the event of such discharge.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Section 10.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote>
                  </para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="20221004">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">The <ital>Discharge and Recovery Reporting</ital>, c. B.1.1, of the <ital>The Saskatchewan Environmental Code</ital>, sets out the requirements for reporting discharges and discoveries of substances that may cause or are causing an adverse effect. <ital>The Environmental Management and Protection (Saskatchewan Environment Code Adoption) Regulations</ital> prescribes the form in which reports to <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;11" nep=";;13" hyphenated="n" folio="7-11" backFolio="7-12" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>the Minister, the landowner, the person&apos;s employer, the person in control of the substance and others who may be directly affected by the discharge must be made.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">R.R.S. c. E-10.22, Reg. 2.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote>
                  </para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="20221004">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">
                    <ital>The Environmental Management and Protection Act 2010</ital> applies to greenhouse gases if unintentionally discharged during storage, including discharge from geological sequestration or when greenhouse gases are transported or used as an input in an industrial process.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Section 52(3)(d).</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote>
                  </para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="20221004">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">
                    <ital>The Environmental Management and Protection Act 2010</ital> calls on the minister to keep a record of environmentally impacted sites. The Minister has the power to issue a written direction to a person who owns or occupies an area of land or water that contains a substance that may cause or is causing an adverse effect to provide the Minister with a report. Reports on environmentally impacted sites are to be in the form prescribed by s. 11 of the Act and any requirements set out in the code. <ital>The Environmental Management and Protection Act 2010</ital>, allows the Minister to require a person who is or may be a &ldquo;person responsible&rdquo; (as defined by the Act) to conduct a site assessment if the Minister reasonably believes that a site may be an environmentally impacted site.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Section 13(1).</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote> Division 5 of this Act directs the minister to establish and maintain a registry of environmentally impacted sites.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Section 22(1).</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Section 5 directs the Minister to prepare a report, to be known as the State of Environment Report, every two years. This report must identify and present analyses respecting how the environment is changing and identify emerging concerns for the environment.</para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="20221004">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">Although none of the current regulations promulgated under <ital>The Environmental Management and Protection Act 2010</ital> are specifically directed at climate change issues, certain sections of the Act and certain regulations promulgated under the Act have incidental effects on total GHG emission in that they restrict certain activities and require licensing of certain proponents and developments. Such regulations include the Halocarbon Control and The Industrial Source (Air Quality) chapters of <ital>The Saskatchewan Environmental Code</ital>, <ital>The Hazardous Substances and Waste Dangerous Goods Regulations</ital>, <ital>The Scrap Tire Management Regulations, 2017</ital>, and <ital>The Household and Paper Stewardship Program Regulations</ital> each of which apply to certain GHGs.</para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">When <ital>The Environmental Management and Protection Act, 2010</ital> was proclaimed the permit-based system outlined in the previous <ital>Environmental Management Protection Act, 2002</ital> for managing the environmental impacts was replaced by the <ital>Saskatchewan Environmental Code</ital>. <ital>The Management and Reduction of Greenhouse Gases Act</ital>, Bill 95, partially proclaimed in January 2018, provides authority for electricity and reporting regulations. An amended Act was proclaimed in full in December 2018, which provided authority for the output-based emissions management framework, including compliance options such as the technology fund.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Saskatchewan, &ldquo;Legislation and Regulations&rdquo; (16 January 2019), online: Saskatchewan <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                              <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3294b022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/business/environmental-protection-and-sustainability/a-made-in-saskatchewan-climate-change-strategy/legislation-and-regulation</ref>
                          </cite>.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote>
                  </para.text>
                </para>
                <head align="l" toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <name date.updated="0">
                      <bold>
                        <ital>The Clean Air Act</ital>
                      </bold>
                    </name>
                  </name.block>
                </head>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">
                    <ital>The Clean Air Act</ital> was repealed by c. E-10.22, <ital>The Environmental Management and Protection Act, 2010</ital> (effective June 1, 2015). Much of <ital>The Clean Air Act</ital>, as it was then, is contained in Part VII of <ital>The Environmental Management and Protection Act, 2010</ital>, entitled &ldquo;Air Quality&rdquo; and in Chapters E.1.1 &ldquo;Halocarbon Control&rdquo; and E.1.2 &ldquo;Industrial Source (Air Quality)&rdquo; of the <ital>Saskatchewan Environmental Code.</ital>
                  </para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="20221004">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">The chapters prohibit the operation or alteration of an industrial source, an incinerator or fuel-burning equipment in a manner that affects the emission of air contaminants, without first obtaining a permit. The &ldquo;Industrial Source (Air Quality)&rdquo; chapter does not apply in general to oil and gas activities regulated pursuant to <ital>The Oil and Gas Conservation Act</ital> and <ital>The Pipelines Act, 1998</ital>, equipment using commercially available fuel for building heating, recreation or food preparation; fuel-burning equipment for road and railway maintenance, seeding, harvesting fertilizing or controlling pests or weeds, intensive livestock operations; on-road or off-road motor vehicles, rail locomotives, boats or aircrafts, or industrial source facilities no longer in operation. <ital>The Environmental Management and Protection Act, 2010</ital> defines &ldquo;air contaminant&rdquo; as any solid, liquid, gas or combination of any of them in the ambient air that may cause or is causing an adverse effect. This leaves open the possibility of a regulation or mechanism under this Act for dealing with GHGs. It also raises the question as to whether GHG emissions may already be considered to be causing an adverse effect.</para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;13" nep=";;15" hyphenated="n" folio="7-13" backFolio="7-14" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>
                  <para.text date.updated="0">Apart from permitting powers, <ital>The Environmental Management and Protection Act, 2010</ital> allows the minister to issue control orders<footnote date.trace="20221004" date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="20221004">9</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Section 54(1).</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> requiring any person who contravenes the &ldquo;Air Quality&rdquo; Part of the Act or the regulations made for the purposes of this Part of the Act to:</para.text>
                  <list list.style="block" date.updated="0">
                    <list.item date.updated="0">
                      <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                        <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                      </name.block>
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">refrain from emitting air contaminants;</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </list.item>
                    <list.item date.updated="0">
                      <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                        <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                      </name.block>
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">control the emission of air contaminants in accordance with directions issued in the order;</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </list.item>
                    <list.item date.updated="0">
                      <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                        <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                      </name.block>
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">comply with procedures to be followed in relation to the emission;</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </list.item>
                    <list.item date.updated="0">
                      <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                        <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                      </name.block>
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">install, replace or alter equipment;</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </list.item>
                    <list.item date.updated="0">
                      <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                        <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                      </name.block>
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">measure the rate of emission of air contaminants;</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </list.item>
                    <list.item date.updated="0">
                      <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                        <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                      </name.block>
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">measure the concentration of air contaminants in the ambient air;</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </list.item>
                    <list.item date.updated="0">
                      <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                        <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                      </name.block>
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">provide information, statistics and other data;</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </list.item>
                    <list.item date.updated="0">
                      <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                        <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                      </name.block>
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">measure or monitor meteorological conditions;</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </list.item>
                    <list.item date.updated="0">
                      <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                        <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                      </name.block>
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">carry out studies for investigations in response to specific air pollution problems;</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </list.item>
                    <list.item date.updated="0">
                      <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                        <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                      </name.block>
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">refrain from removing, repairing, servicing, replacing or altering any equipment designed to control halocarbon discharge;</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </list.item>
                    <list.item date.updated="0">
                      <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                        <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                      </name.block>
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">remove or discharge a halocarbon from a container;</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </list.item>
                    <list.item date.updated="0">
                      <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                        <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                      </name.block>
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">store a halocarbon in a specified manner; and</para.text>
                      </para>
                    </list.item>
                    <list.item date.updated="0">
                      <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                        <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                      </name.block>
                      <para date.updated="0">
                        <para.text date.updated="0">report to the director on any of the above.<footnote date.trace="20221004" date.updated="0">
                            <footnote.reference date.updated="20221004">10</footnote.reference>
                            <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                              <para date.updated="0">
                                <para.text date.updated="0">Section 54(2).</para.text>
                              </para>
                            </footnote.body>
                          </footnote>
                        </para.text>
                      </para>
                    </list.item>
                  </list>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="20221004">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">Where a person fails to carry out the requirements of a control order, the work may be completed by the Minister and the costs recovered against the party to whom the order was issued.<footnote date.trace="20221004" date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="20221004">11</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Section 60.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Where the Minister is of the opinion that it is in the public&apos;s interest to take immediate action to remedy, minimize, mitigate or prevent an adverse effect, or where the minister is unable to identify or locate the person to whom an order should be directed, the Minister may take immediate action to take any measure required by the order.</para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">
                    <bold>Bill 147 - </bold>
                    <bold>
                      <ital>The Oil and Gas Conservation Amendment Act</ital>
                    </bold>
                  </para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">In November 2018, the Government of Saskatchewan introduced <ital>The Oil and Gas Amendment Act </ital>to facilitate emissions regulations in the oil and gas sector. The Regulation of flared and vented methane emissions in the upstream oil and gas sector led to a 71 per cent reduction in emissions from 2015 levels by 2024. The Regulations were amended in 2024 to remove coverage of flared gas, due to its coverage under the OBPS Program, and now focus only on vented gas.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">
                          </para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote>
                    <footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Saskatchewan, &ldquo;Legislation and Regulations,&rdquo; (accessed 25 May 2026), online: <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                              <ref normalpub="webref">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/Business/Environmental-Protection-and-Sustainability/A-Made-in-Saskatchewan-Climate-Change-Strategy/Legislation-and-Regulations</ref>
                            </cite>
                          </para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote>
                  </para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">
                    <bold>
                      <ital>The Management and Reduction of Greenhouse Gases Act</ital>
                    </bold>
                  </para.text>
                </para>
              </section.body>
            </section>
            <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
              <para.text date.updated="0">
              <ital>The Management and Reduction of Greenhouse gases Act </ital>was partially proclaimed in January 2018 to provide authority for electricity and reporting regulations. An amended Act was proclaimed in full in December 2018, which provided the authorities for the output-based emissions management framework, including compliance options such as the Technology Fund, which allows the Minister to support initiatives in Saskatchewan that lead to mitigation,</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
              <para.text date.updated="0">sequestration or capture of greenhouse gases.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
              <para.text date.updated="0">
                <bold>
                  <ital>The Management and Reduction of Greenhouse Gases (Standards and Compliance) Regulations, 2023</ital>
                </bold>
              </para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
              <para.text date.updated="0">Effective January 1, 2023, The Act was amended to create <ital>The Management and Reduction of Greenhouse Gases (Standards and Compliance) Regulations, 2023</ital> which establish parameters for mandatory greenhouse gas reductions through output-based performance standards for industry, along with compliance options, under Saskatchewan&apos;s OBPS Program.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
              <para.text date.updated="0">The OBPS program creates individual standards for Industrial, Electrical, and Aggregate Facilities, along with Performance Credit Standards and a Carbon Capture, Utilization and Storage Credit Standard. The electricity compliance under the Output-Based Performance Standards (OBPS) Program enabled the creation of a Clean Electricity Transition Grant and a Small Modular Reactor Investment Fund in the province.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
              <para.text date.updated="0">
                <bold>
                  <ital>The Management and Reduction of Greenhouse Gases (Reporting and General) Regulations, 2025</ital>
                </bold>
              </para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
              <para.text date.updated="0">Effective June 1, 2025,<bold>
              </bold>additional amendments were made to <ital>The Management and Reduction of Greenhouse Gases Act. </ital>Chapter M-2.01 Reg 5, or <ital>The Management and Reduction of Greenhouse Gases (Reporting and General) Regulations, 2025</ital> were introduced requiring every person who owns or operates a facility that emits 10,000 tonnes or more of CO2e each year to be subject to these regulations by reporting their emissions.</para.text>
            </para>
            <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
              <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                <metadata field="classification">
                  <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
                </metadata>
              </metadata.block>
              <section.front date.updated="0">
                <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                  <designator date.updated="0">7:9</designator>
                  <name date.updated="0">Environment<sep/>Environmental Assessment Legislation</name>
                </outline.name.block>
              </section.front>
              <section.body date.updated="0">
                <head align="l" toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <name date.updated="0">
                      <bold>
                        <ital>The Environmental Assessment Act</ital>
                      </bold>
                      <footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">S.S. 1979-80, c. E-10.1, as amended by the <ital>Statutes of Saskatchewan</ital>, 1983 c.77; 1988-89 c.42 and c.55; 1996 c.F-19.1; 2002, c.C-11.1; 2010, c.11; 2013, c.27; and 2018, c.42.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </name>
                  </name.block>
                </head>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">
                    <ital>The Environmental Assessment Act</ital> outlines requirements for environmental assessment of any &ldquo;development&rdquo; that, <ital>inter alia</ital>, is likely to &ldquo;cause the emission of any pollutant&rdquo;<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Section 2(d)(iii).</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote> or &ldquo;have a significant impact on the environment&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Section 2(d)(vi).</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote> As a result of recent amendments, project proponents first apply to the Minister for a determination of whether their projects are developments and, thus, within the scope of the Act.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Section 7.2(1).</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Approval, and therefore, an assessment, is only required if the project is deemed by the Minister to be a development.</para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">Project proponents may obtain screening advice regarding expected impact of the proposed development on the environment and as to its relative significance from the Environmental Assessment Branch of the Ministry of Environment. The Branch reviews basic information about the project, such as the scale of the project and processes involved and the proposed location of the project, to create an Initial Environmental Evaluation conducted through the Saskatchewan Environmental Assessment Review Panel (&ldquo;SEARP&rdquo;). SEARP is made up of representatives of various government departments and agencies who are assigned to review proposals on behalf of their respective departments or deputy ministers to determine whether an Environmental Impact Assessment (&ldquo;EIA&rdquo;) is required. In this process, the Branch may request that the proponent conduct public information meetings in order to identify public concerns.</para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="20221004">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">If, after consultation with the SEARP, the Branch determines that an EIA is not likely required, the project may proceed if it is developed in accordance with the proposal and so long as it meets all other legal requirements. If an EIA is required, the Branch will prepare project-specific guidelines to provide the proponent with a detailed description of the information that will be required in addition to the department&apos;s general EIA <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;15" nep=";;17" hyphenated="n" folio="7-15" backFolio="7-16" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>guidelines. Project-specific guidelines are subject to public comment for a period of 30 days after they are issued by the Minister of Environment.</para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">The proponent is responsible for conducting the EIA according to the project-specific guidelines and submitting an EIS for the project, the environment, possible alternatives and evaluating potential affects of the development on the environment along with mitigative measures and potential residual impacts. The Branch, in cooperation with SEARP, and sometimes the federal government, coordinates a technical review and provides any preliminary technical review comments in relation to additional studies or additional information required to complete the EIS. When the Branch is satisfied with the content of the EIS, it will complete its review and issue final technical review comments to support final decision-making. Upon completion of the technical review comments, the comments and the EIS are released for public comment for a minimum of 30 days. Following the period of public comment, the Minister issues a decision as to whether or not the development is approved, with or without conditions.</para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">Whether climate change impacts are considered in the environmental assessment process will depend upon the nature of the proposed development and its likely environmental effects.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="20221004">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">For a discussion of how climate change considerations may be incorporated into environmental assessment processes, see the <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                              <ref ref.id="I0a112ca0496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Federal-Provincial-Territorial Committee on Climate Change and Environmental Assessment, Incorporating Climate Change Considerations in Environmental Assessment: General Guidance for Practitioners (November 2003)</ref>
                            </cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                              <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief341b5022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.canada.ca/en/impact-assessment-agency/services/policy-guidance/incorporating-climate-change-considerations-environmental-assessment-general-guidance-practitioners.html</ref>
                          </cite>.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote>
                  </para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan is a party to the Canada-Saskatchewan Agreement on Environmental Assessment Cooperation,<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="20221004">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Canada-Saskatchewan Agreement on Environmental Assessment Cooperation, 2005, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                              <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief34426022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.canada.ca/en/impact-assessment-agency/corporate/acts-regulations/legislation-regulations/canada-saskatchewan-agreement-environmental-assessment-cooperation-2005.html</ref>
                          </cite> (accessed March 2020).</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> which establishes a mechanism for the joint review and evaluation of proposed developments having federal and provincial impacts.</para.text>
                </para>
              </section.body>
            </section>
            <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
              <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                <metadata field="classification">
                  <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
                </metadata>
              </metadata.block>
              <section.front date.updated="0">
                <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                  <designator date.updated="0">7:10</designator>
                  <name date.updated="0">Environment<sep/>Wildlife Protection Legislation</name>
                </outline.name.block>
              </section.front>
              <section.body date.updated="0">
                <head align="l" toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <name date.updated="0">
                      <bold>
                        <ital>The Wildlife Habitat Protection Act</ital>
                      </bold>
                      <footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">S.S. 1983-84, c. W-13.2, as amended by the <ital>Statutes of Saskatchewan</ital>, 1984-85-86, c.3 and 59; 1988-89, c.39; 1992, c.66; 1996, c.70; 1999, c.30; 2002, c.40; 2004, c.69; 2005, c.9 and 31; 2006, c.12; 2007, c.44; 2008, c.28; 2009, c.33; 2010, c.36; and 2018, c.42.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </name>
                  </name.block>
                </head>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">
                  <ital>The Wildlife Habitat Protection Act</ital> is the legislative embodiment of a conservation process initiated by the Government of Saskatchewan in 1984 under what was then <ital>The Critical Wildlife Habitat Protection Act.</ital> Initially, the legislation protects some 3.4 million acres of uplands or wetlands in their natural state. The Act contains restrictions on the sale of designated Crown lands and on the clearing, breaking or drainage of leased Crown land. Use for grazing and haying by cattle producers continues, as does the ability to undertake oil and gas exploration and drilling, provided that appropriate protective measures are employed.</para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="20221004">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">In 2014, however, the provincial government removed a 1.3 million acres of land from these protections by reclassifying them as lands of &ldquo;moderate&rdquo; ecological value. These lands became eligible for sale with the proviso that the land would remain subject to ecological protections through the use of conservation easements. An additional 525,000 acres were eligible for sale without restriction.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Andrea Olive, &ldquo;Under Threat. 20 Years Since the Saskatchewan Wildlife Act&rdquo; (Canadian Centre for Policy Alternatives, February 2018), available at <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                              <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0a1153b2496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6">https://www.policyalternatives.ca/sites/default/files/uploads/publications/Saskatchewan&percnt;20Office/2018/02/Under&percnt;20Threat.pdf</ref>
                          </cite> (accessed October 2019).</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                  </footnote> Consequently, the positive climate benefits from protecting land from breaking, draining, and clearing are at risk.</para.text>
                </para>
                <head align="l" toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <name date.updated="0">
                      <bold>
                        <ital>The Wildlife Act, 1998</ital>
                      </bold>
                      <footnote date.updated="0">
                        <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                        <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">S.S. 1998, c. W-13.12.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </footnote.body>
                      </footnote>
                    </name>
                  </name.block>
                </head>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">This Act vests all property and wildlife in Saskatchewan in the provincial Crown. The Act establishes a scheme for the protection and management of wildlife, the administration of hunting and trapping licences, the imposition of penalties for the commission of offences, and the protection of species at risk. The categories related to species protection in this Act are similar to categories used by the Committee on the Status of Endangered Wildlife in Canada (&ldquo;COSEWIC&rdquo;). The Act contains prohibitions against the disturbance, capture, killing <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;17" nep=";;19" hyphenated="y" folio="7-17" backFolio="7-18" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>and trafficking of protected species. It also provides for the development and implementation of recovery plans in relation to designated species.</para.text>
                </para>
              </section.body>
            </section>
            <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
              <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                <metadata field="classification">
                  <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
                </metadata>
              </metadata.block>
              <section.front date.updated="0">
                <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                  <designator date.updated="0">7:11</designator>
                  <name date.updated="0">Environment<sep/>Product and Building Standards</name>
                </outline.name.block>
              </section.front>
              <section.body date.updated="0">
                <head align="l" toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <name date.updated="0">
                      <bold>
                        <ital>Building Codes</ital>
                      </bold>
                    </name>
                  </name.block>
                </head>
                <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">The 2015 National Building Code of Canada took effect on January 1, 2018 in Saskatchewan as the basis for the province&apos;s building standards. The enforcement of provisions aimed at improving energy efficiency standards for houses and small buildings was delayed until 2019. Saskatchewan implements a province-wide adoption of the National Building Code of Canada through regulations with some amendments.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Canada, &ldquo;Model code adoption across Canada&rdquo; (July 2, 2020), online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                              <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief34b79022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://nrc.canada.ca/en/certifications-evaluations-standards/codes-canada/model-code-adoption-across-canada</ref>
                          </cite>.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote> Updates occur on a five-year cycle. Publication of the 2020 National Building Code of Canada was delayed due to the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic along with the complexity of the proposed changes.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Canada, &ldquo;Codes Canada &ndash; frequently asked questions&rdquo; (July 23, 2021), online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                              <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief34dea022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://nrc.canada.ca/en/certifications-evaluations-standards/codes-canada/codes-canada-frequently-asked-questions</ref>
                          </cite>.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote> However, a significant change includes the creation of energy performance tiers for buildings and houses to set out a model for achieving greater energy efficiency.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Canada, &ldquo;National Building Code of Canada 2020&rdquo; (March 28, 2022), online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                              <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief34dea122c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://nrc.canada.ca/en/certifications-evaluations-standards/codes-canada/codes-canada-publications/national-building-code-canada-2020</ref>
                          </cite>.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote> Likewise, large buildings only became subject to the 2017 National Energy Code of Canada for Buildings in 2019.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">S.S. 1983-84, c. U-1.2, as amended.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote> This Act is binding upon the Crown and is administered by every Saskatchewan municipality. Further updates occurred in 2020.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Canada, &ldquo;National Energy Code of Canada for Buildings 2020&rdquo; (March 28, 2022), online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                              <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3505b022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://nrc.canada.ca/en/certifications-evaluations-standards/codes-canada/codes-canada-publications/national-energy-code-canada-buildings-2020</ref>
                          </cite>.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote>
                  </para.text>
                </para>
              </section.body>
            </section>
          </section.block>
        </analytical.level.body>
      </analytical.level>
    </analytical.level.body>
  </analytical.level>
  <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
    <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
      <metadata field="classification">
        <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
      </metadata>
    </metadata.block>
    <front date.updated="0">
      <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
        <designator date.updated="0">IV</designator>
        <name date.updated="0">Climate Change Policy Development</name>
      </outline.name.block>
    </front>
    <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
      <section.block>
        <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
          <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
            <metadata field="classification">
              <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
            </metadata>
          </metadata.block>
          <section.front date.updated="0">
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
              <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
              <designator date.updated="0">7:12</designator>
              <name date.updated="0">Pre-Kyoto Policy Development</name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body date.updated="0">
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">Early policy development in Saskatchewan was initially driven by electricity conservation concerns. In 1992 the government established the Saskatchewan Energy Conservation and Development Authority (&ldquo;SECDA&rdquo;). SECDA was given the mandate to evaluate and report on electrical generation operations and to promote energy conservation, alternative energy and related business opportunities.<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                          <ref ref.id="I0a117ac0496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan, &ldquo;Saskatchewan Energy Conservation and Development Authority to be Wound&rdquo;, News Release (March 26, 1996)</ref>
                        </cite>, available at <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                          <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0a117ac1496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/government/news-and-media/1996/march/28/saskatchewan-energy-conservation-and-development-authority-to-be-wound</ref>
                        </cite>; and see, for example, <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                          <ref ref.id="I0a117ac2496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan Energy Conservation and Development Authority, &ldquo;Evaluation and Recommendations for Saskatchewan&apos;s Electric Options 2003 to 2020&rdquo; (1994)</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote>
              </para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">In the build-up to Canada&apos;s ratification of the Kyoto Protocol, climate change programs were also initiated by the two large Crown-owned energy utilities, SaskPower, the supplier of electricity in Saskatchewan, and SaskEnergy, the Crown natural gas utility. These Crown corporations have often been used as instruments of provincial government policy.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="20221004">
              <para.text date.updated="0">In 1995, SaskEnergy, with its transmission subsidiary, Trans Gas Ltd., issued an action plan under the Voluntary Climate Change Challenge Program.<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                          <ref ref.id="I0a117ac3496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">SaskEnergy Incorporated and Trans Gas Limited, Action Plan: A Voluntary Climate Change Program (SaskEnergy, 1995)</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
              </footnote> The SaskEnergy/Trans Gas voluntary actions were directed toward cost-effective measures to reduce transmission losses, especially at compressor stations, and energy conservation initiatives with consumers. SaskPower has also been a participant in the Voluntary Change Registry since 1995; however, the program no longer seems to exist.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="20221004">
              <para.text date.updated="0">By 1996, SECDA was disbanded as part of government cost-reduction efforts and most of its responsibilities and remaining programs were transferred to the Saskatchewan Research Council.<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                          <ref ref.id="I0a117ac4496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan, &ldquo;Saskatchewan Energy Conservation and Development Authority to be Wound&rdquo;, News Release (March 26, 1996)</ref>
                        </cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                          <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief357ae022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/government/news-and-media/1996/march/28/saskatchewan-energy-conservation-and-development-authority-to-be-wound</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote>
              </para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
        <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
          <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
            <metadata field="classification">
              <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
            </metadata>
          </metadata.block>
          <section.front date.updated="0">
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
              <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
              <designator date.updated="0">7:13</designator>
              <name date.updated="0">Policy Development in Early Kyoto Era</name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body date.updated="0">
            <para date.updated="20221004">
              <para.text date.updated="0">In 1998, the Government of Saskatchewan established the Saskatchewan Stakeholder Advisory Committee on Climate Change. The Advisory Committee was established to permit government, agriculture, environmental, industry and com<?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;19" nep=";;21" hyphenated="y" folio="7-19" backFolio="7-20" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>munity stakeholders to exchange information related to climate change matters.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">In May of 2001, the Government of Saskatchewan announced the development of a Green Print for Energy Conservation to provide residents of Saskatchewan with a long-term provincial energy conservation strategy.<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                          <ref ref.id="I0a11a1d0496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan, &ldquo;Saskatchewan Green Print for Energy Conservation Backgrounder&rdquo; (Saskatchewan, 2001)</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
              </footnote> The objective of the Green Print was to provide a plan for dealing with escalating energy costs by assisting consumers in managing energy costs in the short-term while establishing a longer-term strategy on energy conservation for Saskatchewan. Although the focus of the Green Print was on the reduction of energy costs, it was also intended to deal with Saskatchewan&apos;s responsibilities and commitments on climate change.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">In response to and in anticipation of federal climate change initiatives, Saskatchewan put forward its own perspective on climate change policy in 2002.<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                          <ref ref.id="I0a11a1d1496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan, &ldquo;Making it Work: A Saskatchewan Perspective on Climate Change Policy&rdquo; (Saskatchewan Industry and Resources, October 2002)</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
              </footnote> That policy recognized anthropogenic climate change and the likelihood of climate change impacts for the Province of Saskatchewan. The Policy emphasized Saskatchewan&apos;s ongoing efforts to implement actions to reduce greenhouse gas emissions, which, at the time, included the following:</para.text>
              <list list.style="block" date.updated="0">
                <list.item date.updated="20221004">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                  </name.block>
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">the establishment of the Saskatchewan Stakeholder Advisory Committee on Climate Change;</para.text>
                  </para>
                </list.item>
                <list.item date.updated="0">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                  </name.block>
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">the enactment of legislation to mandate the use of ethanol in gasoline;</para.text>
                  </para>
                </list.item>
                <list.item date.updated="0">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                  </name.block>
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">the establishment of the Office of Energy Conservation in 2002;</para.text>
                  </para>
                </list.item>
                <list.item date.updated="20221004">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                  </name.block>
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">the initiation of the IEA Weyburn Carbon Dioxide Monitoring and Storage Project;</para.text>
                  </para>
                </list.item>
                <list.item date.updated="0">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                  </name.block>
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">the establishment of the International Test Centre for Carbon Dioxide Capture at the University of Regina;</para.text>
                  </para>
                </list.item>
                <list.item date.updated="20221004">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                  </name.block>
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">support for the establishment of a Prairie Adaptation Network and the Prairie Adaptation Research Collaborative (PARC) which funds adaptation research across the Canadian Prairies;</para.text>
                  </para>
                </list.item>
                <list.item date.updated="0">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                  </name.block>
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">participation in the Gull Lake and Cypress Wind Power Development;</para.text>
                  </para>
                </list.item>
                <list.item date.updated="0">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                  </name.block>
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">the establishment of a forest carbon reserve with SaskPower;</para.text>
                  </para>
                </list.item>
                <list.item date.updated="0">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                  </name.block>
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">various initiatives with the Saskatchewan Research Council and other groups.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </list.item>
              </list>
              <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan&apos;s policy was focused on five major areas:</para.text>
              <list list.style="block" date.updated="0">
                <list.item date.updated="0">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                  </name.block>
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">public education;</para.text>
                  </para>
                </list.item>
                <list.item date.updated="0">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                  </name.block>
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">carbon capture technology;</para.text>
                  </para>
                </list.item>
                <list.item date.updated="0">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                  </name.block>
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">the development of adaptation strategies;</para.text>
                  </para>
                </list.item>
                <list.item date.updated="0">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                  </name.block>
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">the development of biological carbon sinks in agricultural soils and forests; and</para.text>
                  </para>
                </list.item>
                <list.item date.updated="0">
                  <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                    <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                  </name.block>
                  <para date.updated="0">
                    <para.text date.updated="0">energy conservation and alternative energy projects.</para.text>
                  </para>
                </list.item>
              </list>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
        <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
          <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
            <metadata field="classification">
              <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
            </metadata>
          </metadata.block>
          <section.front date.updated="0">
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
              <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
              <designator date.updated="0">7:14</designator>
              <name date.updated="0">Post-Kyoto Policy Development</name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body date.updated="0">
            <para date.updated="20221004">
              <para.text date.updated="0">In 2002, the Government of Saskatchewan created the Office of Energy Conservation. The Office, which was funded by SaskPower, SaskEnergy and Saskatchewan Industry and Resources, was given the purpose of undertaking energy conservation initiatives to reduce energy costs for Saskatchewan residents, businesses and farmers. Again, the emphasis was on energy conservation with a secondary goal of GHG reductions.</para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
        <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
          <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
            <metadata field="classification">
              <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
            </metadata>
          </metadata.block>
          <section.front date.updated="0">
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
              <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
              <designator date.updated="0">7:15</designator>
              <name date.updated="0">Post-Kyoto Policy Development<sep/>A First Report on Making Saskatchewan a Canadian Leader in Energy Conservation and Renewable Energy Development (2006)<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                          <ref ref.id="I0a11a1d2496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Peter Prebble, MLA, &ldquo;A First Report on Making Saskatchewan a Canadian Leader in Energy Conservation and Renewable Energy Development&rdquo; (Government of Saskatchewan, December 2006)</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote>
              </name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body date.updated="0">
            <para date.updated="20221004">
              <para.text date.updated="0">In May 2006, then Premier Lorne Calvert appointed Saskatchewan MLA Peter Prebble as Legislative Secretary for Renewable Energy Development and Conservation and tasked him with the preparation of a report dealing with Saskatchewan&apos;s options for the development of renewable energy sources and energy conservation programs. This built upon the vision of the government of the day that by 2020, one third of Saskatchewan&apos;s energy needs would be met by renewable energy sources. The product of this investigation was <ital>Renewable Energy Development and Conservation in Saskatchewan: A First Report on Making Saskatchewan a Canadian Leader in Energy Conservation and Renewable Energy Development.</ital> The purpose of the report was to provide direction to the govern<?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;21" nep=";;23" hyphenated="n" folio="7-21" backFolio="7-22" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>ment in achieving those ends. The report contained recommendations which would build upon Saskatchewan&apos;s existing wind power, ethanol production, CO<sub>2</sub> sequestration and energy conservation programs. Its focus was primarily on the electricity sector and the buildings sector.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">In the electrical sector, the priority recommendations were focused on renewable energy production and energy conservation through demand management. The report proposed that Saskatchewan&apos;s Renewable Energy and Conservation Portfolio Standard would require that a minimum of 50&percnt; of Saskatchewan&apos;s electricity would be derived from renewable energy sources and electricity conservation by 2025.<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                          <ref ref.id="I0a11c8e0496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Peter Prebble, MLA, &ldquo;A First Report on Making Saskatchewan a Canadian Leader in Energy Conservation and Renewable Energy Development&rdquo; (Government of Saskatchewan, December 2006), at p. 1</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
              </footnote> It was proposed that major wind power developments would contribute 500 MW of electricity to the Saskatchewan system by 2015. Some additional savings would be achieved by encouraging net metering for small-scale producers of &ldquo;green, low impact&rdquo; energy.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">Facing forecasted electricity consumption growth and a need to retrofit or replace existing generating facilities, considerable emphasis in the report is placed on demand management.<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                          <ref ref.id="I0a11c8e1496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Peter Prebble, MLA, &ldquo;A First Report on Making Saskatchewan a Canadian Leader in Energy Conservation and Renewable Energy Development&rdquo; (Government of Saskatchewan, December 2006), at pp. 19-24</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
              </footnote> Measures were proposed to forecast the consumption by 2025 in order to manage the supply gap.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">The second policy priority proposed in the report was financial assistance to promote energy conservation and the development of renewable energy sources. Emphasis was placed on programs to create incentives for energy efficiency in businesses and among consumers.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">The third priority was the conservation of electrical energy and incentives for the use of renewable energy in the commercial and institutional building sector. Proposals included the implementation of an energy efficiency building code standard for new commercial buildings, the promotion of the construction of net zero energy buildings, with emphasis on commercial and institutional buildings and promotion of the adoption of solar energy, with a call for the introduction of &ldquo;solar rights&rdquo; legislation.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">The final priority policies were directed toward the promotion of conservation and renewable energy development. Under this policy-oriented priority, the report called for the expansion of the role of the Office of Energy Conservation and the establishment of an Energy Conservation and Renewable Energy Fund to provide stable funding for conservation and renewable energy initiatives.<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                          <ref ref.id="I0a11c8e2496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Peter Prebble, MLA, &ldquo;A First Report on Making Saskatchewan a Canadian Leader in Energy Conservation and Renewable Energy Development&rdquo; (Government of Saskatchewan, December 2006), at p. 3</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote>
              </para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
        <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
          <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
            <metadata field="classification">
              <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
            </metadata>
          </metadata.block>
          <section.front date.updated="0">
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
              <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
              <designator date.updated="0">7:16</designator>
              <name date.updated="0">Post-Kyoto Policy Development<sep/>The Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007</name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body date.updated="0">
            <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
              <para.text date.updated="0">In 2007, Saskatchewan&apos;s NDP government introduced an aggressive plan to reduce per capita GHG emissions by 32&percnt;, based on 2004 GHG emission levels, by 2020. The plan called for a further reduction in the following years, so as to achieve an 80&percnt; per capita reduction from 2004 levels by 2050. This reduction translated to a 22 tonne per capita reduction in GHG emissions by 2020 (i.e., a target reduction from 71.9 tonnes of GHG per person recorded in 2004 to 49.9 tonnes of GHG per person in 2020)<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Jose Candor, &ldquo;Options to Implement a Regulatory Framework to Accommodate Geological Storage in CO<sub>2</sub> in Saskatchewan&rdquo;, ScienceDirect (2013), online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                          <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief37018022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://reader.elsevier.com/reader/sd/pii/S1876610213009569?token&equals;CCF1E49E94BBCE299E8FF994A25AC625452D3C86AAB5A1FF5BB8F3AFAD9A4DED3C4BE4ADEE63883AF7742C382775D558&amp;originRegion&equals;us-east-1&amp;originCreation&equals;20220613002309</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote> and a per capita reduction of 55 tonnes by 2050 (i.e., a target of 16.9 tonnes of GHG per person in 2050). This was expressed by the government of the day as &ldquo;the most ambitious target in Canada&rdquo;<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007, at p. 3.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote> and as being significantly greater than the percentage reduction required for Canada to meet the reduction required under the Kyoto Protocol.<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007, at p. 7.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
              </footnote> The plan was promoted as particularly ambitious because of Saskatchewan&apos;s cold climate, its energy-intensive economy and its rapid economic growth. Ultimately, Saskatchewan abandoned the Plan, and this goal was not met; Saskatchewan&apos;s per capita emissions for 2020 were recorded at 55.8 tonnes.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">Borrowing language from former U.S. Vice-President Al Gore, the Plan described Saskatchewan as representing a &ldquo;tipping point&rdquo; in that, despite its small population, Saskatchewan&apos;s efforts to curb GHG emissions would make a genuine <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;23" nep=";;25" hyphenated="n" folio="7-23" backFolio="7-24" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>difference on the world stage. The Plan recognized the challenges faced by a province having only 3&percnt; of Canada&apos;s population but which contributed, as of 2004, 9&percnt; of Canada&apos;s GHG emissions. It also recognized the continuation of the province&apos;s rapid economic growth was largely dependent on the continued growth of the natural resource sector, especially fossil fuel based energy.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">The Plan was based on five &ldquo;wedges&rdquo; or areas of emission reduction and climate change policy: conservation and energy efficiency; carbon capture and storage in the oil and gas and electricity sectors; increased use of renewable energy; promotion of the creation of natural carbon sinks; and the reduction of methane and other emissions from the oil and gas and agricultural sectors. The Plan placed significant reliance upon the development of as yet unproven technology in such areas as carbon capture and sequestration in order to meet its ambitious targets, especially for the post-2020 period. As such, considerable emphasis was given to innovation in the energy sector, the promotion of efficiency and conservation, the creation of alternatives to fossil fuels and measures to remove carbon dioxide from the environment.</para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
        <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
          <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
            <metadata field="classification">
              <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
            </metadata>
          </metadata.block>
          <section.front date.updated="0">
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
              <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
              <designator date.updated="0">7:17</designator>
              <name date.updated="0">Post-Kyoto Policy Development<sep/>The Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007<sep/>Conservation and Efficiency</name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body date.updated="0">
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">The Plan included a commitment to save <ital>at least</ital> 300 MW of SaskPower&apos;s electricity generating capacity through demand side management practices, thereby eliminating the equivalent of one coal-fired generating plant.<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                          <ref ref.id="I0a11eff1496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007, at p. 10</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
              </footnote> Whether this was intended to mean the closure of a coal-fired plant, or whether the purpose was to simply free SaskPower to export more electricity was not answered by the Plan. Part of the energy savings were intended to be realized through existing programs that encouraged individuals and families to save electricity through better household management, better insulation and more efficient heating and a retail sales tax exemption for energy-efficient appliances. Other savings would be realized from existing programs that assisted communities, commercial and industrial facilities, farms and municipalities.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="20221004">
              <para.text date.updated="0">The Plan identified a series of other continuing initiatives. SaskPower would begin a transition to smart meters. Government would promote energy-efficient products and practices by setting new standards, codes and regulations. New technologies would be promoted, such as home energy monitors, in order to make consumers better aware of their energy consumption and to better plan for energy consumption reductions. The Plan reaffirmed a commitment to reduce energy consumption from government&apos;s core buildings by 20&percnt;, to be realized through the replacement of building components.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">The Plan also called for incremental measures in the area of conservation and efficiency. Proposed immediate action included the implementation of a government vehicle purchasing policy requiring all vehicles to be hybrid-electric, alternative or flex fuel, or within the top 20&percnt; of efficiency in their class. As an alternative to constructing new electricity generating facilities, SaskPower, a traditional vehicle of government policy, would introduce programs to reduce its electrical load by 300 MW by 2017. Eligibility criteria for the Investment Tax Credit for Manufacturing and Processing would be expanded to include more types of renewable energy and energy conservation equipment used to generate electricity. A voluntary, province-wide vehicle retirement program was proposed to support the existing federal program to retire older vehicles. Finally, energy conservation and efficiency initiatives in the agricultural sector were to be supported.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">On the two-year planning horizon, the Plan called for the establishment of a Climate Change Secretariat with increased funding and responsibility for the coordination of climate change policy and the delivery of energy conservation programs. It also called for the implementation of new energy efficiency building standards, in consultation with industry.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">On a five-year planning horizon, the Plan targeted continued funding of programs for homeowners that would help them reduce energy consumption, maximize the development of small-scale, low environmental impact local electricity generation in northern Saskatchewan and consult with residents of the North as to their long-term requirements for power supply and transmission infrastructure.</para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
        <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
          <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
            <metadata field="classification">
              <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
            </metadata>
          </metadata.block>
          <section.front date.updated="0">
            <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;25" nep=";;27" hyphenated="y" folio="7-25" backFolio="7-26" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
              <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
              <designator date.updated="0">7:18</designator>
              <name date.updated="0">Post-Kyoto Policy Development<sep/>The Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007<sep/>Carbon Capture and Storage in the Oil and Gas and Electricity Sectors<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                          <ref ref.id="I0a11eff2496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007, at pp. 11-12</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote>
              </name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body date.updated="0">
            <para date.updated="20221004">
              <para.text date.updated="0">The Plan built upon Saskatchewan&apos;s leading role in the study of carbon dioxide sequestration in geological formations in the province, in particular the Weyburn oilfield. When injected in deep, oil-bearing formations, carbon dioxide can be used for purposes of enhanced oil recovery. This gives rise to the somewhat ironic result of greater production of fossil fuels for export. Saskatchewan also intended to rely upon storage and other geological formations containing salt water. Saline aquifers underlie much of the southern half of the province. This was seen as affording an opportunity for low-cost, highly reliable storage of carbon dioxide emissions captured from coal-fired power plants, heavy oil upgraders and fertilizer plants.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">In order to develop the technology necessary to embark upon this extensive implementation of carbon dioxide capture and storage, Saskatchewan proposed to continue to support its Petroleum Technology Research Center and the International Test Center for Carbon Dioxide Capture, also located in the province. In 2014, the province completed the construction of a carbon capture facility: the Boundary Dam Integrated Carbon Capture and Storage (CCS) Demonstration Project.</para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
        <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
          <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
            <metadata field="classification">
              <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
            </metadata>
          </metadata.block>
          <section.front date.updated="0">
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
              <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
              <designator date.updated="0">7:19</designator>
              <name date.updated="0">Post-Kyoto Policy Development<sep/>The Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007<sep/>Increase the Use of Renewable Energy<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                          <ref ref.id="I0a121700496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007, at pp. 12-13</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote>
              </name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body date.updated="0">
            <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
              <para.text date.updated="0">At the time the Plan was released, Saskatchewan had the largest per capita wind power generation capacity of any region in Canada. Saskatchewan also continues to produce a large amount of hydroelectric power, although not enough to meet its entire domestic needs. Saskatchewan has approximately 889 MW of hydroelectric capacity.<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1.50</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Canada, &ldquo;Provincial and Territorial Energy Profiles &ndash; Saskatchewan&rdquo; (July 28, 2022), online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                          <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief37c4d022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.cer-rec.gc.ca/en/data-analysis/energy-markets/provincial-territorial-energy-profiles/provincial-territorial-energy-profiles-saskatchewan.html</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
              </footnote> The Plan supported the addition of small-scale, environmentally friendly, electric power generation from independent power producers.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="20221004">
              <para.text date.updated="0">In 2005, Saskatchewan became the first province to mandate the use of ethanol blends in gasoline. In 2020-2021, Saskatchewan was home to four commercial-level ethanol plants. In 2020, Canada had eight operational bio-diesel plants.<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">U.S. Department of Agriculture, Foreign Agricultural Service, &ldquo;Biofuels Annual: Canada&rdquo; (March 4, 2022), online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                          <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief37ebe022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://apps.fas.usda.gov/newgainapi/api/Report/DownloadReportByFileName?fileName&equals;Biofuels&percnt;20Annual_Ottawa_Canada_06-14-2021.pdf</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote> As of 2015, one bio-diesel plant was in operation in Saskatchewan.<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Canadian Renewable Fuels Association, &ldquo;Canadian Ethanol Facilities&rdquo; (June 14, 2021), online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                          <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief37ebe122c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://ricanada.org/wp-content/uploads/2015/03/Canadian-Ethanol-and-Biodiesel-Facilities-Producer-Tables-for-Website.pdf</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote> A new bio-diesel plant is expected to be constructed in Saskatchewan by 2027.<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Mickey Djuric, &ldquo;&dollar;2B renewable diesel, canola-crushing plant to be built in Regina&rdquo;, CBC News (January 17, 2022), online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                          <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3812f022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/saskatchewan/diesel-canola-plant-regina-1.6318248&num;:&tilde;:text&equals;The&percnt;20Saskatchewan&percnt;20goverment&percnt;20says&percnt;20the,to&percnt;20be&percnt;20completed&percnt;20by&percnt;202027.&amp;text&equals;Saskatchewan&percnt;20Premier&percnt;20Scott&percnt;20Moe&percnt;20called,largest&percnt;20investments&percnt;20in&percnt;20the&percnt;20province</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
              </footnote> The ethanol plants produce grain-based ethanol for blending with conventional gasoline fuel. Saskatchewan has supported the investigation of alternative ethanol production technologies, including cellulose-based ethanol. It has also funded research for the promotion of other alternative fuels, including hydrogen. The Plan called for the establishment of a Bio-Products Centre for Excellence and cooperation with industry to increase sales of ethanol-blended gasoline by 2010. It hoped for the development of a 1.4 billion litre biofuels industry, with resultant job creation benefits, the development of hydrogen production and utilization pilot projects (including fuel station infrastructure) and the implementation of the government vehicle purchasing policy discussed above.</para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
        <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
          <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
            <metadata field="classification">
              <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
            </metadata>
          </metadata.block>
          <section.front date.updated="0">
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
              <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
              <designator date.updated="0">7:20</designator>
              <name date.updated="0">Post-Kyoto Policy Development<sep/>The Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007<sep/>Promoting the Creation of Natural Carbon Sinks<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                          <ref ref.id="I0a121701496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007, at p. 13</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote>
              </name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body date.updated="0">
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">The Plan assumed a national emissions trading system in Canada that would facilitate the promotion of natural carbon sinks. Saskatchewan would achieve global GHG reductions by encouraging farmers to increase agricultural soil sinks (target<?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;27" nep=";;29" hyphenated="y" folio="7-27" backFolio="7-28" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>ing the removal of 25 Mt of CO<sub>2</sub> per year by 2012, increasing to more than 37 MT by 2050). Additional CO<sub>2</sub> emission reductions would be achieved by the reforestation of 20,000 hectares of land by 2017 (estimated to result in another 4.9 Mt in CO<sub>2</sub> emission reductions).</para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
        <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
          <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
            <metadata field="classification">
              <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
            </metadata>
          </metadata.block>
          <section.front date.updated="0">
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
              <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
              <designator date.updated="0">7:21</designator>
              <name date.updated="0">Post-Kyoto Policy Development<sep/>The Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007<sep/>Reduction of Methane and Other Emissions from Oil, Gas and Agricultural Sectors<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                          <ref ref.id="I0a121702496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007, at p. 14</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote>
              </name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body date.updated="0">
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">In agriculture, the Plan targeted emissions from livestock production and the inefficient use of nitrogen fertilizer. Its goal was to develop more efficient management practices and technology that would result in a 20&percnt; reduction in agricultural emissions per animal and per acre of crop production by 2030.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">In the oil and gas sector, Saskatchewan committed to work with industry to prepare a report and recommendations for the improvement of the industry&apos;s efficiency and competitiveness, and to reduce emissions from flaring and venting, along with fugitive emissions, which together make up a very significant share of the province&apos;s total GHG emissions.</para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
        <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
          <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
            <metadata field="classification">
              <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
            </metadata>
          </metadata.block>
          <section.front date.updated="0">
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
              <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
              <designator date.updated="0">7:22</designator>
              <name date.updated="0">Post-Kyoto Policy Development<sep/>The Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007<sep/>Sustainable Non-Renewable Resource Sector<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                          <ref ref.id="I0a121703496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007, at pp. 15-16</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote>
              </name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body date.updated="0">
            <para date.updated="20221004">
              <para.text date.updated="0">As the second-largest oil-producing province in Canada and the third-largest producer of coal and natural gas, Saskatchewan&apos;s goal is to continue to reap the economic benefits from these activities, while at the same time recognizing that they account for producing over half of the province&apos;s GHG emissions. The Plan saw technology as the principal means by which these emissions could be reduced, principally carbon capture and sequestration, clean coal, polygeneration technology and the reduction of flaring and venting and fugitive emissions from the oil and gas sector. The second tactic was the development of new energy resources, such as oil sands, oil shale and natural gas from coal (although one would speculate that any increase in production in these areas would also increase GHG emissions).</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="20221004">
              <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan is either the largest or second-largest producer of uranium.<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">See Government of Saskatchewan, &ldquo;Energy&rdquo;, online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                          <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief38af3022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/business/investment-and-economic-development/key-economic-sectors/energy</ref>
                        </cite> and Government of Canada, &ldquo;About Uranium&rdquo; (September 23, 2020), online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                          <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief38af3122c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.nrcan.gc.ca/energy/energy-sources-distribution/uranium-nuclear-energy/uranium-canada/about-uranium/7695</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
              </footnote> The Plan called for assessment of the development potential of a nuclear reactor to supply SaskPower with electricity for domestic and export markets.</para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
        <section date.trace="1">
          <section.front>
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y">
              <label>&sect;</label>
              <designator>7:23.60</designator>
              <name>Saskatchewan&apos;s Affordable Power Plan &ndash; Related Initiatives</name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body>
            <para date.trace="1">
              <para.text>The province also points to complementary measures, including Saskatchewan&apos;s Critical Minerals Strategy, launched in March 2023, to double the number of critical minerals produced by 2030 and support the transition to a lower carbon economy. Moreover, the establishment of the Saskatchewan Research Council Rare Earth Processing Facility in Saskatoon will allow rare earth elements to be processed and used in clean electricity technologies. Lastly, the province has continued to work on the 2021 CCUS priorities, including CCUS credits introduced in 2023 to encourage OBPS-regulated emitters to adopt CCUS technology.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.trace="1">
              <para.text>Saskatchewan also frames part of its contribution to global emissions reductions as supplying international markets with lower-emission products and promoting sustainable land use, progressive environmental regulation, and innovation to support climate resilience and sustainable economic development.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">13</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Saskatchewan, &ldquo;Saskatchewan&apos;s Climate Change Strategy,&rdquo; online: <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                          <ref normalpub="webref">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/business/environmental-protection-and-sustainability/a-made-in-saskatchewan-climate-change-strategy/saskatchewans-climate-change-strategy</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote>
              </para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
        <section date.trace="1">
          <section.front>
            <outline.name.block>
              <label>&sect;</label>
              <designator>7:23.50</designator>
              <name>Saskatchewan&apos;s Affordable Power Plan &ndash; Funding</name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body>
            <para>
              <para.text>The affordable Power Plan uses OBPS compliance payments from the electricity sector to support SaskPower&apos;s clean grid transition, splitting proceeds so that half goes to an SMR Investment Fund and the other half is provided to SaskPower through the Clean Electricity Transition Grant (CETG) to support clean electricity operating costs, including renewable power imports, demand-side management programs, and renewable power purchase agreements In 2024-25, the Ministry of Environment allocated &dollar;140 million in CETG funding to SaskPower.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para>
              <para.text>Between April 1, 2024, and March 31, 2025, the CETG is reported to have enabled 3,737,117 MWh of renewable generation, which is enough to power approximately 480,000 homes for a year. Additionally, it supported 1,754 household efficiency retrofits, including 62 in northern Indigenous communities, and helped 30 businesses access technical and financial support to implement energy-saving measures.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">12</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Saskatchewan, &ldquo;Saskatchewan&apos;s Climate Change Strategy,&rdquo; online: <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                          <ref normalpub="webref">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/business/environmental-protection-and-sustainability/a-made-in-saskatchewan-climate-change-strategy/saskatchewans-climate-change-strategy</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote>
              </para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
        <section date.trace="1">
          <section.front>
            <outline.name.block>
              <label>&sect;</label>
              <designator>7:23.40</designator>
              <name>Saskatchewan&apos;s Affordable Power Plan</name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body>
            <para>
              <para.text>Saskatchewan&apos;s Affordable Power Plan outlines initiatives to achieve a net-zero electricity grid by 2050, positioning that timeline as realistic while aiming to maintain affordable, reliable electricity for households and businesses as the grid decarbonizes.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para>
              <para.text>A variety of energy sources will continue to be used in their approach to meet growing demand, as a mix of coal, natural gas, and renewables such as wind and solar will ensure dependable baseload power while transitioning toward future nuclear baseload generation.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">11</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Saskatchewan, &ldquo;Saskatchewan&apos;s Climate Change Strategy,&rdquo; online: <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                          <ref normalpub="webref">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/business/environmental-protection-and-sustainability/a-made-in-saskatchewan-climate-change-strategy/saskatchewans-climate-change-strategy</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote>
              </para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
        <section date.trace="1">
          <section.front>
            <outline.name.block>
              <label>&sect;</label>
              <designator>7:23.30</designator>
              <name>Prairie Resilience: A Made-in-Saskatchewan Climate Change Strategy &ndash; OBPS Outcomes</name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body>
            <para>
              <para.text>Reported outcomes of the OBPS program include &dollar;3.3 billion in savings for regulated emitters from 2019&ndash;2022 and projected savings of &dollar;14 billion by 2030 compared to federal carbon pricing. Additionally, full compliance by regulated emitters under the OBPS program has been observed, and the Saskatchewan Technology Fund awarded &dollar;25 million to 13 projects at its first intake in 2023. Together, the projects will eliminate more than 4.5 million tonnes of CO2e emissions, attract over &dollar;277 million in additional investment, and save five million gigajoules of energy. A second intake announced January 16, 2025 made &dollar;50 million available for additional industry-driven projects.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Saskatchewan, &ldquo;Saskatchewan&apos;s Climate Change Strategy,&rdquo; online: <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                          <ref normalpub="webref">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/business/environmental-protection-and-sustainability/a-made-in-saskatchewan-climate-change-strategy/saskatchewans-climate-change-strategy</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote>
              </para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
        <section date.trace="1">
          <section.front>
            <outline.name.block>
              <label>&sect;</label>
              <designator>7:23.20</designator>
              <name>Prairie Resilience: A Made-in-Saskatchewan Climate Change Strategy &ndash; Key Programs</name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body>
            <para>
              <para.text>The Prairie Resilience strategy introduced two key programs to reduce emissions in the province. The Methane Action Plan and the Output-Based Performance Standards (OBPS) Program have worked together to reduce upstream oil and gas venting and flaring emissions by 71&percnt; since 2015.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para>
              <para.text>The OBPS Program is Saskatchewan&apos;s industrial emissions framework, designed to reduce emissions intensity while preventing carbon leakage. Changes effective January 1, 2023, increased reduction requirements, expanded coverage, and introduced a Carbon Capture Utilization and Storage (CCUS) credit program aimed at further incentivizing reductions in industrial emissions. Facilities that exceed permitted levels must use performance or CCUS credits or pay into the Saskatchewan Technology Fund, which supports emissions-reduction projects at regulated facilities.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Saskatchewan, &ldquo;Saskatchewan&apos;s Climate Change Strategy,&rdquo; online: <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                          <ref normalpub="webref">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/business/environmental-protection-and-sustainability/a-made-in-saskatchewan-climate-change-strategy/saskatchewans-climate-change-strategy</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote>
              </para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
        <section date.trace="1">
          <section.front>
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y">
              <label>&sect;</label>
              <designator>7:23</designator>
              <name>Prairie Resilience: A Made-in-Saskatchewan Climate Change Strategy</name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body>
            <para>
              <para.text>Prairie Resilience is Saskatchewan&apos;s climate change strategy, launched in December 2017, aimed at reducing greenhouse gas emissions while protecting competitiveness, prioritizing affordability, and supporting sustainable growth and development. It is framed as a practical approach to strengthening the province&apos;s resilience to climate impacts while recognizing the role of a strong, sustainable economy in addressing climate change. The strategy reduces emissions through agricultural practices such as carbon sequestration via zero and low tillage, improved fertilizer efficiency through 4R nutrient stewardship, and methane reductions using innovative animal feed.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Saskatchewan, &ldquo;Saskatchewan&apos;s Climate Change Strategy,&rdquo; online: <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                          <ref normalpub="webref">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/business/environmental-protection-and-sustainability/a-made-in-saskatchewan-climate-change-strategy/saskatchewans-climate-change-strategy</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote>
              </para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
        <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
          <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
            <metadata field="classification">
              <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
            </metadata>
          </metadata.block>
          <section.front date.updated="0">
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
              <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
              <designator date.updated="0">7:23</designator>
              <name date.updated="0">Post-Kyoto Policy Development<sep/>The Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007<sep/>Adaptation<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                          <ref ref.id="I0a123e10496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007, at pp. 17-18</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote>
              </name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body date.updated="0">
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">In preparation for warmer, drier weather and potentially more severe climate events including drought, heat waves, storm flooding and tornadoes, the Plan&apos;s focus was on working with other agencies to develop adaptation strategies. Much of the focus was on working with the Prairie Adaptation Research Collaborative, the Saskatchewan Research Council and the Saskatchewan Forestry Centre to develop a provincial adaptation strategy. The Plan also called for the integration of climate change impacts and adaptation and to risk assessments of business and strategic plans of all Crown corporations and departments. Expanded watershed planning to protect vulnerable water resources was looked upon as a means to allow for specific adaptation measures to be developed at a very local level.</para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
      </section.block>
    </analytical.level.body>
  </analytical.level>
  <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
    <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
      <metadata field="classification">
        <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
      </metadata>
    </metadata.block>
    <front date.updated="0">
      <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
        <designator date.updated="0">V</designator>
        <name date.updated="0">Current Legal and Policy Response</name>
      </outline.name.block>
    </front>
    <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
      <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
        <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
          <metadata field="classification">
            <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
          </metadata>
        </metadata.block>
        <front date.updated="0">
          <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
            <designator date.updated="0">A</designator>
            <name date.updated="0">Mitigation</name>
          </outline.name.block>
        </front>
        <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
          <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <designator date.updated="0">1</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Major Initiatives</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </front>
            <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
              <section.block>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                    <metadata field="classification">
                      <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
                    </metadata>
                  </metadata.block>
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:24</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">The Management and Reduction of Greenhouse Gases Act<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">S.S. 2010, c. M-2.01, as amended by the <ital>Statutes of Saskatchewan</ital>, 2013, c.13; 2014, c.E-13.1; and 2018, c.48.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">On May 11, 2009, the Government of Saskatchewan intro<?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;29" nep=";;31" hyphenated="n" folio="7-29" backFolio="7-30" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>duced Bill 95, <ital>The Management and Reduction of Greenhouse Gases Act</ital> (&ldquo;<ital>MRGGA</ital>&rdquo;). It was touted as establishing &ldquo;a made-in-Saskatchewan plan for reducing GHG emissions to meet provincial targets and promote investments in low-carbon technologies&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                  <ref ref.id="I0a137690496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan, &ldquo;Saskatchewan Takes Real Action to Reduce Greenhouse Gas Emissions&rdquo;, News Release (May 11, 2009)</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                      </footnote> It filled the void left when Saskatchewan abandoned <ital>Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan, 2007</ital> introduced by the previous government.</para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">This legislation was reintroduced with minor amendments and assented to in 2010. It was finally proclaimed in 2018 with significant amendments as part of the province&apos;s climate strategy, <ital>Prairie Resilience.</ital> The <ital>MRGGA</ital> provides the overarching regulatory framework to implement the current government&apos;s climate change strategy, <ital>Prairie Resilience.</ital>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">The <ital>MRGGA</ital> empowers the province to set GHG emission reduction targets and to monitor and enforce compliance with them.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Part II.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> The <ital>MRGGA</ital> then targets &ldquo;regulated emitters&rdquo; by requiring them to establish baseline emission levels for each of their facilities and then to reduce those emissions over time.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Part VI, Division 1.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> Reduction requirements are specified in a series of regulations emission standards passed pursuant to the <ital>MRGGA.</ital>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                    <metadata field="classification">
                      <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
                    </metadata>
                  </metadata.block>
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:25</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Regulated Emitters (General)</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>The Management and Reduction of Greenhouse Gases (Standards and Compliance) Regulations</ital>
                        <footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Chapter M-2.01 Reg. 3, as amended by Saskatchewan Regulations 91/2019 and 103/2020.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> defines a regulated emitter as an owner or operator of a facility that emitted at least 25,000 tonnes of carbon dioxide equivalent in 2017. Emitters with over 10,000 tonnes but less than 25,000 tonnes can apply to be a regulated emitter.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Section 3.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> Regulated emitters are required to be registered<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Section 5.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> and determine their baseline emission level and intensity for each product they produce with the assistance of a &ldquo;qualified person&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Section 13.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> They are then required to reduce their emissions based on the rate of total allowable annual emissions as outlined in the Standard established for that sector.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Sections 11 and 15.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> The Minister is empowered to set up offset programs to assist emitters in meeting their annual emission targets.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Section 20.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> Finally, regulated emitters are required to regularly file emissions returns to verify compliance with the <ital>MRGGA.</ital>
                        <footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Part V.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>Prairie Resilience</ital> explains that more than 40 industrial facilities will be subject to emission reductions as they qualify as regulated emitters under <ital>The Management and Reduction of Greenhouse Gases (Standards and Compliance) Regulations.</ital>
                        <footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="20221004">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                  <ref ref.id="I0a139da0496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan, Prairie Resilience: Output-Based Performance Standards (December 2017)</ref>
                                </cite>, available at: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief39999022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/-/media/news-release-backgrounders/2018/aug/prairieresilience-performancestandard.pdf</ref>
                              </cite> (accessed October 2019).</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> An overall 10&percnt; reduction or 5.3 million tonnes of carbon dioxide equivalent GHGs will be achieved by 2030 through output-based performance standards that will be increased over time.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="20221004">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                  <ref ref.id="I0a139da2496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan, &ldquo;New Industry Standards Next Step in Prairie Resilience Climate Change Strategy&rdquo;, News Release (August 29, 2018)</ref>
                                </cite> available at <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief39c0a022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/government/news-and-media/2018/august/29/prairie-resilience-climate-strategy</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> The overall reduction will be achieved through 5&percnt; intensity reductions in the potash, coal and uranium mining, iron, steel and pulps mills, and fertilizer and ethanol production.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="20221004">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                  <ref ref.id="I0a139da4496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan, &ldquo;New Industry Standards Next Step in Prairie Resilience Climate Change Strategy&rdquo;, News Release (August 29, 2018)</ref>
                                </cite> available at <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief39e7b022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/government/news-and-media/2018/august/29/prairie-resilience-climate-strategy</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:26</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Electricity Producers</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Large electricity producers, however, are subject to their own regulations under <ital>The Management and Reduction of Greenhouse Gases (General and Electricity Producer) Regulations.</ital>
                        <footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Chapter M-2.01, Reg 1.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> The &ldquo;regulated emitter&rdquo; under these regulations includes any person who produces in excess of 1.5 million tonnes of GHGs while generating electricity from gas or coal-fired generation and independent power producers (IPPs).<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Section 2.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> IPPs are defined as those who generate over 10,000 tonnes per year of carbon dioxide equivalent GHGs from sources other <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;31" nep=";;33" hyphenated="n" folio="7-31" backFolio="7-32" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>than biomass and flaring or venting gas. Like other regulated emitters, large electricity producers and IPPs are required to submit emission returns<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Section 7.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> and are subject to the administrative penalties specified in the <ital>MRRGA</ital> if they fail to comply with these regulations.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Appendix, Part 3.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> These regulations impose a cumulative emissions cap of 64.5 million tonnes of carbon dioxide equivalent by 2029.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Part 4.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> This cap, combined with the promise to shift 50&percnt; of electricity generation to renewable power, are expected to result in a 40&percnt; annual reduction below 2005 emission levels.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan, Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan 2007, at p. 6.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                    <metadata field="classification">
                      <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
                    </metadata>
                  </metadata.block>
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:27</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">The Saskatchewan Technology Fund</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>The Management and Reduction of Greenhouse Gases Act</ital> has created the Saskatchewan Technology Fund &ldquo;to support initiatives in Saskatchewan that lead to mitigation, sequestration or capture of greenhouse gases&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Section 23.1(2)(a).</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> Although the fund is administered by the Minister, it may have an appointed advisory committee to assist the Minister in determining how the fund should be used and administered.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Section 23.2.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> Revenue in the fund will be generated from carbon compliance payments and any administrative penalties that may be assessed.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Section 23.3.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                    <metadata field="classification">
                      <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
                    </metadata>
                  </metadata.block>
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:28</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Administrative, Inspections and Enforcement</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Part VIII of the <ital>The Management and Reduction of Greenhouse Gases Act</ital> establishes the procedures by which the Minister can require the production of information, undertake inspections and pursue investigations and maintain copies of records.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                    <metadata field="classification">
                      <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
                    </metadata>
                  </metadata.block>
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:29</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Administrative, Inspections and Enforcement<sep/>Offences and Administrative Penalties</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">As per s. 75(2) of <ital>The Management and Reduction of Greenhouse Gases Act</ital>, every person who contravenes a provision of the Act, the regulations or the codes is guilty of an offence and is liable on summary conviction to a fine not exceeding &dollar;1 million. A person or entity will be in violation of the Act if a false statement or false information is provided, if one omits to state a fact that is required or that is necessary to make a statement not misleading, if one fails to comply with an order of the Minister, or fails to comply with any provision of the Act, the regulations or the codes.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Section 75(1).</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> Any director, office or agent of a corporation who directed, authorized, assented to or acquiesced in or participated in an act or omission of the corporation that would constitute an offence by the corporation is also guilty of an offence and liable to prosecution.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Section 75(3).</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> A court will be able to impose additional penalties by prohibiting the convicted person from doing any act or engaging in any activity that, in the opinion of the court, may result in the continuation of the offence.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="20221004">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Section 75(4)(a).</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> The Minister will be empowered to impose administrative penalties for non-compliance with the Act. Any person assessed an administrative penalty will have the right to appeal the penalty to the Saskatchewan Court of Queen&apos;s Bench within 30 days after the date of the Minister&apos;s decision to impose the penalty.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Section 79.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">One of the main components of <ital>Prairie Resilience</ital> is the <ital>Methane Action Plan</ital>, which was released in 2019.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Saskatchewan, &ldquo;Methane Action Plan&rdquo; (January 2019), online at: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3a83f022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/business/environmental-protection-and-sustainability/a-made-in-saskatchewan-climate-change-strategy/methane-action-plan&num;:&tilde;:text&equals;Released&percnt;20in&percnt;20January&percnt;202019&percnt;2C&percnt;20the,upstream&percnt;20oil&percnt;20and&percnt;20gas&percnt;20industry</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> This initiative aims to reduce methane-based GHG emissions within the province&apos;s oil and gas sector with the specific goal of reducing carbon dioxide equivalent (CO<sub>2</sub>e) by 4.5 million tonnes annually by 2025.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Saskatchewan Methane Action Plan, online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3a83f122c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://publications.saskatchewan.ca/api/v1/products/92902/formats/109981/download</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> At the point of production, companies often have three options for managing methane, which include flaring (burning) the gas, venting (releasing) the gas into the atmosphere, or capturing the gas and making commercial use of it. Of these options, venting is the most emitting. Capture and commercial use is the most environmentally friendly choice, but it is more economically challenging, as North American <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;33" nep=";;35" hyphenated="n" folio="7-33" backFolio="7-34" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>natural gas prices are low and Saskatchewan lacks province-wide gas-gathering infrastructure. Therefore, <ital>Prairie Resilience</ital> commits to an increase in commercial uses of methane, such as in heating and electricity production, and directs the focus away from venting and flaring.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Saskatchewan, Prairie Resilience, online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3a83f222c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://publications.saskatchewan.ca/api/v1/products/92902/formats/109981/download</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">The <ital>Output-Based Performance Standards</ital> initiative also aims to reduce GHG emissions in the oil and gas sector for large industrial emitters. Regulations apply to facilities that emit over 25,000 tonnes of carbon dioxide equivalents annually and mandate a reduction in production emissions intensity. The goal of this initiative is to record a total emissions intensity reduction of 10&percnt; by 2030.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Saskatchewan, Prairie Resilience, online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3aab0022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://publications.saskatchewan.ca/api/v1/products/91091/formats/107966/download</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">In 2022, the province announced that the Output-Based Performance Standards initiative will expand to encompass five additional sectors, including wood product manufacturing, mineral product manufacturing, chemical manufacturing, agricultural and industrial equipment manufacturing, and food and beverage processing. These sectors will save a total of &dollar;2.3 billion compared to coverage under the federal industrial pricing system. Additionally, more companies are eligible for coverage because the participation threshold has been reduced to 0 tonnes of carbon dioxide equivalent.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">9</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Saskatchewan, &ldquo;Output-Based Performance Standards&rdquo; (19 January 2022), online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3aab0122c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/government/news-and-media/2022/january/19/saskatchewan-expands-obps-to-protect-additional-sectors-from-the-federally-imposed-carbon-tax</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Following the introduction of these initiatives, methane emissions through venting and flaring totaled 5.2 million tonnes (Mt) of carbon dioxide equivalent in 2020. This is almost a 50&percnt; reduction from 2015 levels and is tantamount to removing 1.1 million cars from Saskatchewan roads for one year.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">10</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Saskatchewan, &ldquo;First Annual Oil and Gas Emissions Report&rdquo; (20 December 2021), online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3ad21022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/government/news-and-media/2021/december/20/saskatchewan-releases-first-annual-oil-and-gas-emissions-report</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
              </section.block>
            </analytical.level.body>
          </analytical.level>
          <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
              <metadata field="classification">
                <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
              </metadata>
            </metadata.block>
            <front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <designator date.updated="0">2</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Supplementary Initiatives</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </front>
            <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
              <section.block>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                    <metadata field="classification">
                      <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
                    </metadata>
                  </metadata.block>
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:30</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Renewable Fuels</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">In April 2002, the Government of Saskatchewan released its Greenprint for Ethanol Production in Saskatchewan.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                  <ref ref.id="I0a13ebc0496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Ministry of Industry and Resources, &ldquo;Greenprint on Ethanol Production in Saskatchewan&rdquo; (Saskatchewan Industry and Resources, April 2002)</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> The intent was to create a framework to stimulate the development of an ethanol fuel industry in Saskatchewan. Ethanol was seen as a viable fuel to reduce the dependence of Saskatchewan&apos;s residents upon gasoline and as a means of providing new marketing opportunities for Saskatchewan&apos;s grain producers and wood product companies. The initiative was also seen as a means by which vehicle GHG emissions could be reduced by as much as 30&percnt; where ethanol is blended with gasoline.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                  <ref ref.id="I0a13ebc1496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Ministry of Industry and Resources, &ldquo;Greenprint on Ethanol Production in Saskatchewan&rdquo; (Saskatchewan Industry and Resources, April 2002), at p. 3</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">At the time, Saskatchewan&apos;s ethanol production was approximately 12 million litres per year. Forecasted growth as a result of mandatory ethanol blending of fuels would push required capacity to 130 million litres per year, requiring the construction of additional ethanol production plants. The Greenprint also envisioned a potential export market requiring the resources and providing a marketplace that would produce 400 million litres of fuel per annum. At the time, this was anticipated to create the demand for 1.08 million tonnes of wheat as feedstock, the creation of up to 450 jobs in Saskatchewan and approximately &dollar;272.8 million in construction investment.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                  <ref ref.id="I0a13ebc2496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Ministry of Industry and Resources, &ldquo;Greenprint on Ethanol Production in Saskatchewan&rdquo; (Saskatchewan Industry and Resources, April 2002), at p. 8</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Apart from ethanol-based fuel, the Greenprint also recognized the opportunity to develop a biodiesel industry through the use of animal fats and vegetable oils from crops, including soy beans, canola, corn and sunflowers.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                  <ref ref.id="I0a13ebc3496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Ministry of Industry and Resources, &ldquo;Greenprint on Ethanol Production in Saskatchewan&rdquo; (Saskatchewan Industry and Resources, April 2002), at p. 10</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Building on this initiative, the Saskatchewan government enacted <ital>The Ethanol Fuel Act.</ital>
                        <footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">S.S. 2002, c. E-11.1 (in force July 15, 2002).</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> The Act, as implemented through <ital>The Ethanol Fuel (General) Regulations</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">R.R.S., c. E-11.1, Reg. 1 (in force October 24, 2002), as amended by Saskatchewan Regulations 115/2002, 11/2004, 41/2004, 113/2005, 58/2006, 111/2006, 1/2015 and 27/2020.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> required distributors of automotive gasoline fuel to blend ethanol with <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;35" nep=";;37" hyphenated="n" folio="7-35" backFolio="7-36" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>unleaded automotive gasoline fuel in a manner such that the average volume of the ethanol blend would have had an average composition of at least 1.0&percnt; ethanol for the period November 1, 2005 to ending on January 14, 2007.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">R.R.S., c. E-11.1, Reg. 1, s. 5(1).</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                      </footnote> This increased to 7.5&percnt; ethanol by December 31, 2007 and remains here for all unleaded automotive gasoline fuel.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                    <metadata field="classification">
                      <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
                    </metadata>
                  </metadata.block>
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:31</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Renewable Energy<sep/>Nuclear Energy</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">In 2008, the Government of Saskatchewan established the Uranium Development Partnership for the purpose of examining and reporting upon uranium resources in the Province of Saskatchewan (which was the world&apos;s largest producer of uranium at the time) and also to examine exploration, mining, upgrading, power generation, used fuel management, research and development in Saskatchewan. The Uranium Development Partnership submitted its report to government on March 31, 2009.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                  <ref ref.id="I0a13ebc4496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">&ldquo;Capturing the Full Potential of the Uranium Value Chain in Saskatchewan&rdquo; (March 31, 2009)</ref>
                                </cite>, available at <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0a1412d0496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6">www.leaderpost.com/pdf/udp_final_report.pdf</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">The Report included several key findings in relation to nuclear power generation.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                  <ref ref.id="I0a1412d1496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">&ldquo;Capturing the Full Potential of the Uranium Value Chain in Saskatchewan&rdquo; (March 31, 2009)</ref>
                                </cite>, available at <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0a1412d2496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6">www.leaderpost.com/pdf/udp_final_report.pdf</ref>
                              </cite>, at p. 7.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> Electricity demand growth in Saskatchewan is expected to create a need for between 1,200-1,750 MW by 2020 and between 2,200-3,000 MW by 2030. There may also be export opportunities of between 4,000 and 5,000 MW of electricity to Alberta by 2020. Of this, approximately 3,000 MW of nuclear capacity could be constructed in Saskatchewan. The Report recommended inclusion of nuclear energy as part of Saskatchewan&apos;s long-range energy planning mix. As of 2019, Saskatchewan generated 24.1 terawatt-hours (TWh) of electricity.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2.50</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Government of Canada, &ldquo;Provincial and Territorial Energy Profiles &ndash; Saskatchewan&rdquo; (28 July 2022), online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3b956022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.cer-rec.gc.ca/en/data-analysis/energy-markets/provincial-territorial-energy-profiles/provincial-territorial-energy-profiles-saskatchewan.html</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">As a follow-up to the Report, the government established a Future of Uranium in Saskatchewan Public Consultation Process. The Consultation Process was established to carry out public consultations in June of 2009 and focussed on the recommendations contained in the Report. The public consultations revealed that a majority of Saskatchewan residents viewed nuclear energy positively, but nuclear energy proved to be a polarizing issue.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                  <ref ref.id="I0a1412d3496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Sylvia Fedoruk Canadian Centre for Nuclear Innovation, &ldquo;Who do you trust? University of Saskatchewan survey looks at nuclear perceptions&rdquo;, News Release (May 14, 2014)</ref>
                                </cite>, available at <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0a1412d4496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6">https://news.usask.ca/media-release-pages/2014/who-do-you-trust-u-of-s-survey-looks-at-nuclear-perceptions.php</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">In 2018, Saskatchewan joined National Resources Canada initiative to develop a roadmap for the possible introduction and regulation of small module nuclear reactors in Canada.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Canadian Nuclear Association, &ldquo;SMR Roadmap&rdquo;, available at <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0a1412d5496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6">https://smrroadmap.ca</ref>
                              </cite> (accessed October, 2019).</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> Current Premier Scott Moe has clearly stated that tapping into Saskatchewan&apos;s abundant uranium resources is one of the strategies the province is considering to reduce provincial GHG emissions by replacing electricity generation from coal.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">David Baxter, &ldquo;Saskatchewan Giving Early Consideration to Small Nuclear Reactors&rdquo;, Global News (May 16, 2019), online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3bbc7022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://globalnews.ca/news/5286649/saskatchewan-small-nuclear-reactors/</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Pursuant to his stated intentions, in 2019 Moe announced that the province would look to small modular reactors (SMRs) in the future as part of its goals to lower carbon emissions.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Jennifer Francis, &ldquo;Green Party leaders urge Sask. government to half small modular reactor plans&rdquo;, CBC News (May 9, 2022), online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3be38022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/saskatchewan/green-party-leaders-urge-sask-to-government-to-halt-snrs-1.6447250</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">This year, 2022, in April the province announced it had gone ahead with the plan and will be partnering with Ontario, New Brunswick and Alberta to develop SMRs, four of which will be in Saskatchewan.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                    <metadata field="classification">
                      <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
                      <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
                    </metadata>
                  </metadata.block>
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:32</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Renewable Energy<sep/>Wind Power</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Wind generation is featured prominently in the <ital>Prairie Resilience</ital> plan to achieve a 40&percnt; reduction in GHG emissions from 2005 levels by increasing total electricity produced by wind to 16&percnt;. Saskatchewan currently has nine operational wind power facilities that account for 3&percnt; of the province&apos;s energy production capacity. Sunbridge Wind Power Facility can produce 11 MW of power and was one of Canada&apos;s first, large-scale wind power projects. Other facilities include Cypress (11 MW), Centennial (150 MW), Morse (23 MW), Red Lily (26 MW), <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;37" nep=";;39" hyphenated="n" folio="7-37" backFolio="7-38" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>Western Lily (20 MW), Riverhurst (10 MW), Blue Hill (175 MW), and Golden South (200 MW).<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">SaskPower, &ldquo;System Map&rdquo;, online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3c0a9022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.saskpower.com/our-power-future/our-electricity/electrical-system/system-map</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> A number of these projects are operated independently and sell the power they produce through contracts with SaskPower.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">SaskPower Annual Report, p. 10.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> With the recent announcement that Potentia Renewables Inc. would be constructing the Golden South Wind Energy Facility, Saskatchewan plans to increase wind generation capacity by 200MW. Golden South Wind Facility, the largest facility of its kind in Saskatchewan to date, includes 50 wind turbines is capable of generating enough power for 100,000 homes.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">SaskPower, &ldquo;Golden South Wind Facility&rdquo;, online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3c0a9122c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.saskpower.com/Our-Power-Future/Our-Electricity/Electrical-System/System-Map/Golden-South-Wind-Facility</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                      </footnote> Golden South is an example of one of the aforementioned projects that are operated independently and sell the power they produce through contracts with SaskPower.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                    <metadata field="classification">
                      <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
                    </metadata>
                  </metadata.block>
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:33</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Renewable Energy<sep/>Solar Energy</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">As part of the province&apos;s commitment in <ital>Prairie Resilience</ital> to increase the amount of electricity generated by renewables to 50&percnt; of the province&apos;s total energy use, a 60 MW solar energy project has been announced. This project constitutes the first major investment by the Province in solar energy infrastructure.</para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">2021 saw the opening of Highfield Solar Facility, the province&apos;s first utility-scale solar generation project. The facility includes nearly 30,000 panels, compared to a typical residential project of roughly 20 panels. Highfield is capable of generating enough power for about 2,500 homes.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">SaskPower, &ldquo;Highfield Solar Facility&rdquo;, online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3c31a022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.saskpower.com/Our-Power-Future/Our-Electricity/Electrical-System/System-Map/Highfield-Solar-Facility</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                    <metadata field="classification">
                      <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
                    </metadata>
                  </metadata.block>
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:34</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Renewable Energy<sep/>Net Metering Program</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Through the Saskatchewan Research Council, wind power, low-impact hydro, biomass, heat reclaim, flare gas and photovoltaic electricity generation projects with generating capacities of 100 kW or less and that comply with the area utility&apos;s net metering policies are eligible for a one-time payment of up to 20&percnt; of the first &dollar;100,000 in start-up costs.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                    <metadata field="classification">
                      <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
                    </metadata>
                  </metadata.block>
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:35</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Energy Efficiency</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan does not have any legislation specific to energy efficiency. Instead, the province has adopted policies directed toward energy efficiency.</para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">In addition to the Building Code requirements discussed in <cite finder="crime" type="secondaryxref">
                          <ref ref.id="I0a1460f0496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" secref="7:11">&sect;7:11</ref>
                      </cite>, the province has adopted various programs to assist commercial enterprises with improving energy efficiency. Currently, the province offers rebates of up to 50&percnt; to replace commercial refrigeration products and to study and implement more efficient ice rinks. The Parking Lot Controller Program provides &dollar;50 per controller installed in parking lots to govern the flow of energy to cars. Finally, the Compressed Air Program provides financial incentives to audit and upgrade compressed air systems.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                    <metadata field="classification">
                      <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
                    </metadata>
                  </metadata.block>
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:36</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Energy Efficiency<sep/>Energy Efficiency&mdash;Programs for Individuals and Home Owners</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">The Government of Saskatchewan had implemented several smaller-scale programs to promote energy efficiency and thereby aid in the reduction of the province&apos;s GHG emissions. Most of these programs, however, have expired without being renewed.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                    <metadata field="classification">
                      <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
                    </metadata>
                  </metadata.block>
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:37</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Transportation</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>Prairie Resilience</ital> does not contain explicit emission reduction targets for transportation even though the sector is responsible for 14&percnt; of the province&apos;s emissions according to data from 2015. Rather the plan commits to the following:<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan, Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan 2007, at p. 7.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                      <list list.style="block" date.updated="0">
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Monitor the highway system for vulnerabilities that could impact human health, property and transportation efficiency to help prioritize preventative maintenance and redesign.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Create a freight strategy to improve delivery times, reduce fuel use, and increase efficiency.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Increase the use of idle time limiters in government trucks to reduce fuel usage.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                        <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;39" nep=";;41" hyphenated="n" folio="7-39" backFolio="7-40" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Evaluate government fleet vehicles for lower-carbon technology opportunities.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Continue to support industry in expanding the size and usage of the short haul (short line) rail systems.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Expand the Trucking Partnership Program to increase fuel efficiency in freight hauling.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Facilitate traffic data specific to Saskatchewan to mitigate emissions from congestion and idling.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                      </list>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                    <metadata field="classification">
                      <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
                    </metadata>
                  </metadata.block>
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:38</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Transportation<sep/>Anti-Idling</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">The transportation programs introduced by the Government of Saskatchewan are small-scale, largely voluntary and local in focus. The Idle Free Zone program is designed to encourage drivers visiting schools, health facilities, municipal offices and community and recreation centres and government offices to turn off their vehicle engines when not driving. The program provides free outdoor signage to schools, health facilities, community and recreation centres and municipal offices.</para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">In addition, the Saskatchewan Ministries of Environment and Government Services have adopted an anti-idling initiative for all government vehicles. The program encourages minimizing idling time and the adoption of driving practices that maximize fuel efficiency.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="20221004">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">See Saskatchewan Ministry of Environment website, at <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3ccde022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/environment&num;utm_campaign&equals;q2_2015&amp;utm_medium&equals;short&amp;utm_source&equals;&percnt;2Fenvironment</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
              </section.block>
            </analytical.level.body>
          </analytical.level>
          <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <designator date.updated="0">3</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Carbon Sequestration</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </front>
            <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
              <section.block>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                    <metadata field="classification">
                      <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
                    </metadata>
                  </metadata.block>
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:39</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Introduction</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Sequestration involves the permanent, verifiable capture of carbon dioxide. With a growing population and economy driven largely by the GHG-producing energy and extractive resource industries and little appetite to implement programs that might impede such growth, Saskatchewan has placed considerable reliance upon carbon sequestration as a means of achieving its GHG reduction targets. On one end of the technology spectrum is agricultural biotic sequestration, capturing CO<sub>2</sub> in plants and soil. On the opposite end of that spectrum is Saskatchewan&apos;s program for the development of large-scale carbon capture and storage projects in the energy sector.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                    <metadata field="classification">
                      <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
                    </metadata>
                  </metadata.block>
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:40</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Biotic Carbon Sequestration</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Opportunities in Saskatchewan for biotic carbon sequestration have been identified in the agricultural and forestry sectors.</para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Prairie Adaptation Research Collaborative (&ldquo;PARC&rdquo;) is a partnership of the governments of Canada, Alberta, Saskatchewan and Manitoba mandated to pursue climate change impacts and adaptation research in the Prairie Provinces. In its research,<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                  <ref ref.id="I0a148800496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Mark Johnston, Ph.D., &ldquo;The Carbon Sink Potential in Saskatchewan: Report of an Expert Workshop held at Prairie Adaptation Research Collaborative&rdquo; (Regina, Sask.: March 25-26, 2002)</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                      </footnote> PARC identifies Saskatchewan as having a large carbon sink potential in its agricultural land base. Most of this potential is on cropland. Varying levels of sequestration could be achieved through initiatives in the reduction of tillage and summer-fallow, introduction of forages in the crop rotation cycle and better use of manure and compost. Agricultural sequestration carries uncertainties as to the duration and persistence of sequestration. There is also the question of adoption by landowners and the economic incentives required to secure widespread adoption.</para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Under a 50-year agreement, Saskatchewan Environment and SaskPower have created the first forest carbon sequestration project to be formally reviewed and approved in Canada under the Greenhouse Gas Emission Reduction Trading (&ldquo;GERT&rdquo;) Pilot. It was determined that the project will result in real, measurable, verifiable and surplus net sequestration. The amount of CO<sub>2</sub>e is calculated as the difference between emissions under the reference (without project) case and the with-project case. The expenditure of some &dollar;6 million for the establishment of white spruce plantations by planting five million seedlings on 3300 ha of land and the creation of 206,000 ha of Forest Carbon Reserves is expected to result in a net sequestration of 1.6 Mt. Reviewers considered a number of issues including leakage, the permanence of the sequestration and risk of losses, measuring the benefit, projecting impacts, sampling and measurement, and accounting methods. GERT specified reporting and other conditions to be fulfilled in order to permit registration of the project.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="legacy" type="periodical">
                                  <ref normalpub="lr" ref.id="I0a148801496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" reftext="Tony C. Lempri&egrave;re, Mark Johnston, Al Willcocks, Bryan Bogdanski, Deb Bisson, Mike Apps, and Oliver Bussler, &ldquo;Saskatchewan Forest Carbon Sequestration Project&rdquo;">Tony C. Lempri&egrave;re, Mark Johnston, Al Willcocks, Bryan Bogdanski, Deb Bisson, Mike Apps, and Oliver Bussler, &ldquo;Saskatchewan Forest Carbon Sequestration Project&rdquo; (2002), 78 Forestry Chronicle 843, at p. 843</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> Nonetheless, rates of zero-tillage have been increasing in Saskatchewan.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                  <ref ref.id="I0a148802496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan Ministry of the Environment, 2017 State of the Environment Report (2017), at 33</ref>
                                </cite>, available at <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0a148803496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/residents/environment-public-health-and-safety/state-of-the-environment/about-the-report1</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                    <metadata field="classification">
                      <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
                    </metadata>
                  </metadata.block>
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;41" nep=";;43" hyphenated="n" folio="7-41" backFolio="7-42" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:41</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Carbon Capture and Storage</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan, primarily through SaskPower, is investing heavily in the development of new industrial carbon dioxide capture and geological sequestration technology. The plan calls for the capture of CO<sub>2</sub> from coal-fired electric generating stations and transportation via pipeline to nearby oilfields and its injection into oil-bearing formations to enhance oil recovery. The captured emissions thereby become a commodity that assists in the recovery of additional petroleum. (Of course, the recovered petroleum will eventually be turned into GHG-generating fuel.)</para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">In 2018, Whitecap Resources Inc. acquired the largest enhanced oil recovery project in Canada, near Weyburn, Saskatchewan.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Whitecap Resources Inc., &ldquo;Weyburn Unit&rdquo;, available at <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0a14af10496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6">https://www.wcap.ca/operations/core-operating-areas/weyburn-unit.html</ref>
                              </cite> (accessed October 2019).</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> Since the year 2000, 36 Mt have been sequestered at the Weyburn Unit.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1.50</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Whitecap Resources: A Responsible Energy Story, online: <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3d6a2022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.wcap.ca/sustainability/co2-sequestration</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> When complete, 40 million tonnes of CO2 will have been captured and stored.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Natural Resources Canada, &ldquo;International Energy Agency Greenhouse Gas Weyburn-Midale CO2 Monitoring and Storage Project&rdquo;, available at <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0a14af11496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6">https://www.nrcan.gc.ca/energy/publications/16459</ref>
                              </cite> (accessed October 2019).</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">In 2006, the Government of Saskatchewan announced an initiative to develop a &dollar;1.5 billion, 300 MW near-zero emissions pulverized coal electricity generating plant that was expected to capture about 8,000 tonnes of CO<sub>2</sub> per day. It was intended to allow SaskPower to meet emerging regulatory requirements and add generating capacity to utilize Saskatchewan&apos;s 300-year supply of low-cost lignite coal.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="20221004">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                  <ref ref.id="I0a14fd30496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Saskatchewan, &ldquo;Saskatchewan-based Partnership Developing World-Leading Clean Coal Technology&rdquo;, News Release (July 6, 2006)</ref>
                                </cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3db84022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/government/news-and-media/2006/august/17/saskatchewanbased-partnership-developing-worldleading-clean-coal-technology&num;:~:text&equals;SASKATCHEWAN&percnt;2DBASED&percnt;20PARTNERSHIP&percnt;20DEVELOPING&percnt;20WORLD&percnt;2DLEADING&percnt;20CLEAN&percnt;20COAL&percnt;20TECHNOLOGY,-Released&percnt;20on&percnt;20August&amp;text&equals;Babcock&percnt;20&percnt;26&percnt;20Wilcox&percnt;20Canada&percnt;20(B&percnt;26W&percnt;20Canada,emissions&percnt;20coal&percnt;2Dfired&percnt;20power&percnt;20plant</ref>
                              </cite>.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                      </footnote> Cost escalation that put the project cost at some &dollar;3.8 billion resulted in the cancellation of the proposed development in 2007.</para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">In 2014, SaskPower completed the Boundary Dam Integrated Carbon Capture and Storage Demonstration Project. The Boundary Dam Project was the world&apos;s first commercially viable large-scale carbon capture and storage project at a coal-fired power station. The Boundary Dam reduces approximately 800,000 tonnes of CO<sub>2</sub> emissions through carbon capture. The Captured CO<sub>2</sub> is transported by pipeline and sold for use in enhanced oil recovery.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                  <ref ref.id="I0a152441496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">SaskPower, Powering Saskatchewan to a cleaner Energy Future: 2016-2017 Annual Report, at 35</ref>
                                </cite>, available at <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0a152442496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6">https://www.saskpower.com/about-us/Our-Company/Current-Reports/Past-Annual-Reports</ref>
                              </cite> (accessed August 2017).</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                      </footnote> Before further investment in carbon capture, however, an evaluation of the Boundary Dam project with be completes because its costs have far exceeded expectations.</para.text>
                    </para>
                    <para date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">The Boundary Dam Project creates opportunities for the sale of other captured by-products. SaskPower currently captures sulphur dioxide and converts it to sulphuric acid for its own use, but intends to begin selling it for industrial use. SaskPower also captures fly-ash, a coal combustion by-product, and sells it for use in ready-mix concrete and other purposes.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                  <ref ref.id="I0a152443496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">SaskPower, Powering Saskatchewan to a cleaner Energy Future: 2016-2017 Annual Report, at 35</ref>
                                </cite>, available at <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0a152444496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6">https://www.saskpower.com/about-us/Our-Company/Current-Reports/Past-Annual-Reports</ref>
                              </cite> (accessed August 2017).</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
              </section.block>
            </analytical.level.body>
          </analytical.level>
          <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
            <front date.updated="0">
              <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                <designator date.updated="0">4</designator>
                <name date.updated="0">Agriculture; Natural Systems; Community Preparedness; Carbon Pricing</name>
              </outline.name.block>
            </front>
            <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
              <section.block>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:42</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Agriculture</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="20221004">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>Prairie Resilience</ital> acknowledges the complicated role agricultural production and agricultural lands play in climate change as both a source and sink of GHGs.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan, Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan 2007, at p. 4.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> Consequently, the province has committed to maintaining the total permanent cover on 19.93 million hectares, the level of permanent cover that existed in 2016, and to maintaining the annual amount of soil organic matter sequestered cultivated at 5.60 million tonnes through the promotion of practices such as zero tillage. Finally, <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;43" nep=";;45" hyphenated="n" folio="7-43" backFolio="7-44" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>the province intends to ensure that 25&percnt; of Saskatchewan&apos;s cropland has a nutrient stewardship plan by 2025.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="20221004">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">
                                <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                                  <ref ref.id="I0a154b51496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Government of Saskatchewan, Saskatchewan&apos;s Climate Resilience Measurement Framework (December 2017)</ref>
                                </cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url">
                                  <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="Ief3e2d7022c311ed945d9977b80060b9">https://www.saskatchewan.ca/business/environmental-protection-and-sustainability/a-made-in-saskatchewan-climate-change-strategy/climate-resilience-framework-and-reports</ref>
                              </cite> (accessed October 2019).</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:43</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Natural Systems</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>Prairie Resilience</ital> highlights the importance of natural systems in helping the province mitigate and adapt to the effects of climate change. The plan, however, contains few details. Rather, the province promises to maintain or improve existing practices including:<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan, Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan 2007, at pp. 4 and 5.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                      <list list.style="block" date.updated="0">
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Ensure natural and commercially forested lands are managed in a manner that enhances the removal and storage of carbon from the atmosphere while allowing for sustainable harvesting, respecting normal forest cycles and fire preparedness.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Maintain or restore landscape integrity to optimize ecological goods and services, enhance resilience to extreme weather events and manage the risk to biodiversity.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Increase our understanding of future climate trends and adaptation options to address impacts that extreme weather events such as flooding and droughts could have on future water supplies.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Advance options to improve Saskatchewan&apos;s ability to measure stream flows and provide flow forecasts to aid in adapting to the potential effects of climate change on watersheds and infrastructure operations.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Continue to implement Saskatchewan&apos;s agricultural water management framework in the province to help assure continued productivity, enhance wetland habitat conservation and improve runoff management in times of both drought and flood.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Advance options to focus on permanent flood damage reduction and flood mitigation projects and thereby increase resiliency of flood prone areas.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Continue to improve the condition and resilience of provincially owned dams and water infrastructure.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                      </list>
                    </para>
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">The only new commitments include creating a carbon offset system for soils and forests and to work with other jurisdictions to establish a coordinated network of parks and conservations areas. There are no timelines, however, associated with these commitments.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:44</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Community Preparedness</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>Prairie Resilience</ital> recognizes that communities need the capacity to respond to the effects of a changing climate. As a consequence, some attention is given to helping build resiliency with First Nations, M&eacute;tis, and municipal communities. As the list of provincial commitments in this area reveals, the emphasis is on examining threats and encouraging these communities to respond rather than taking firm steps to build resiliency:<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan, Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan 2007, at p. 9.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                      <list list.style="block" date.updated="0">
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Examine current floodplain mapping to identify local communities at greatest risk of flooding, fire, and extreme weather events.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Encourage family preparedness plans, by making emergency preparedness guides and suggested emergency kit content lists available through Saskatchewan and local government websites.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Maintain and enhance partnerships with First Nations and M&eacute;tis communities to address and adapt to a changing climate through actions that are guided by traditional ecological knowledge.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Encourage communities to develop appropriate plans and preparedness to respond and recover from extreme weather events.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Encourage municipalities to consider disaster mitigation projects a priority when applying for infrastructure funding.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                        <list.item date.updated="0">
                          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                            <designator date.updated="0">&bull;</designator>
                          </name.block>
                          <para date.updated="0">
                            <para.text date.updated="0">Promote the upgrading of municipal waste and sewage management services to reduce, capture and use GHG emissions and biogas that would otherwise be released into the air, by making these projects a priority category for support under joint federal/provincial funding programs.</para.text>
                          </para>
                        </list.item>
                      </list>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
                <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
                  <section.front date.updated="0">
                    <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;45" nep=";;47" hyphenated="n" folio="7-45" backFolio="7-46" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>
                    <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                      <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                      <designator date.updated="0">7:45</designator>
                      <name date.updated="0">Carbon Pricing</name>
                    </outline.name.block>
                  </section.front>
                  <section.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <ital>Prairie Resilience</ital> makes it clear that the made-in-Saskatchewan response to climate change will not include a carbon tax. It describes the federal carbon tax as ineffective and penalizing Saskatchewan over other regions because of Saskatchewan&apos;s reliance on agriculture and the oil and gas sectors.<footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan, Saskatchewan Energy and Climate Change Plan 2007, at p. 1.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote> The promotion of a carbon tax is cited as the primary reason that Saskatchewan has not signed on to the Vancouver Declaration on Clean Growth and Climate Change. It is not surprising, therefore, that following the release of <ital>Prairie Resilience</ital>, Saskatchewan residents became subject to a federal carbon tax under the <ital>Greenhouse Gas Pollution Pricing Act.</ital>
                        <footnote date.updated="0">
                          <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                          <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                            <para date.updated="0">
                              <para.text date.updated="0">S.C. 2018, c. 12.</para.text>
                            </para>
                          </footnote.body>
                        </footnote>
                      </para.text>
                    </para>
                  </section.body>
                </section>
              </section.block>
            </analytical.level.body>
          </analytical.level>
        </analytical.level.body>
      </analytical.level>
      <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
        <front date.updated="0">
          <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
            <designator date.updated="0">B</designator>
            <name date.updated="0">The Oil and Gas Emissions Management Regulations</name>
          </outline.name.block>
        </front>
        <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
          <section.block>
            <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
              <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
                <metadata field="classification">
                  <value>ENV.III.2.e Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Approvals, licences and orders&mdash;Air</value>
                </metadata>
              </metadata.block>
              <section.front date.updated="0">
                <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
                  <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
                  <designator date.updated="0">7:46</designator>
                  <name date.updated="0">Generally</name>
                </outline.name.block>
              </section.front>
              <section.body date.updated="0">
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">
                    <ital>The Oil and Gas Emissions Management Regulations</ital>
                    <footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Chapter O-2, Reg. 7, as amended by Saskatchewan Regulations 8/2020 and 107/2020.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote> supplement the requirements of the <ital>MRGGA</ital>, but are actually passed pursuant to <ital>The Oil and Gas Conservation Act.</ital>
                    <footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.trace="20221004" date.updated="20221004">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">R.S.S. 1978, c. O-2, as amended by <ital>the Statutes of Saskatchewan</ital>, 1982-83, c.1; 1983, c.54; 1988-89, c.31; 1989-90, c.54; 1990-91, c.39; 1993, c.35; 1998, c.30; 2000, c.50; 2001, c.26; 2003, c.29; 2007, c.7; 2010, c.E-9.22; 2011, c.11; 2014, c.E-13.1 and c.21; 2015, c.21; 2017, c.21; 2018, c.42; and 2019, c.14.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote> They specifically address operations that flare or vent gas with potential emissions that are greater than 50,000 tonnes of carbon dioxide equivalent per year.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Section 3.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote> It also regulates emissions from oil facilities.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Section 8.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote> Each business subject to these regulations is required to have an emissions reduction plan that is approved by the Minister.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Part 4.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote> Failure to comply with that plan is an offence and will result in an administrative penalty that will reach &dollar;50 per tonne of excess emissions by 2030.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Section 10(2) and Table 3.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote> The Minister has discretion to defer the payment of any administrative penalty of the business assessed with the penalty is participating in a &ldquo;qualified conservation project&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">Part 5.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote>
                  </para.text>
                </para>
                <para date.updated="0">
                  <para.text date.updated="0">The <ital>Oil and Gas Emissions Management Regulations</ital> are designed to assist Saskatchewan in meeting the target in the 2017 Pan-Canadian Framework of reducing emissions from the oil and gas sector by 40-45&percnt; by 2025. Success in meeting this target, however, is conditional on the Saskatchewan government creating a market demand for methane. In addition, a 10&percnt; reduction is being set for refining and upgrading and 15&percnt; reduction for upstream combustion in this sector.<footnote date.updated="0">
                      <footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference>
                      <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                        <para date.updated="0">
                          <para.text date.updated="0">
                            <cite finder="legacy" type="secondary">
                              <ref ref.id="I0a159970496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6" short="y">Environment and Climate Change Canada, Canada&apos;s Second Biennial Report on Climate Change, at 38</ref>
                            </cite>, available at <cite finder="legacy" type="url">
                              <ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0a159971496e11eb9821d0218604b8d6">https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/climate-change/greenhouse-gas-emissions/second-biennial-report.html</ref>
                          </cite>.</para.text>
                        </para>
                      </footnote.body>
                    </footnote>
                  </para.text>
                </para>
              </section.body>
            </section>
          </section.block>
        </analytical.level.body>
      </analytical.level>
    </analytical.level.body>
  </analytical.level>
  <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
    <front date.updated="0">
      <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
        <designator date.updated="0">VI</designator>
        <name date.updated="0">Future Trends in Provincial Law and Policy; Regional and International Linkages</name>
      </outline.name.block>
    </front>
    <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
      <section.block>
        <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
          <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
            <metadata field="classification">
              <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
            </metadata>
          </metadata.block>
          <section.front date.updated="0">
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
              <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
              <designator date.updated="0">7:47</designator>
              <name date.updated="0">Future Trends in Provincial Law and Policy</name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body date.updated="0">
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">The future of climate change law and policy in Saskatchewan will involve a complicated balancing act, with a popular and political demand for continued natural resource-led economic growth on one hand and external (national and international) GHG reduction compliance pressures on the other. Saskatchewan&apos;s new-found position as a &ldquo;have&rdquo; instead of a &ldquo;have-not&rdquo; province that is no longer dependent upon federal government transfer payments, the reduction of its reliance on the agricultural sector and its transformation from a sleepy, aging province into a booming, diversified economy and the effects of those changes&mdash;economic and attitudinal&mdash;should not be underestimated. The understandable reluctance to slow the engines of economic and population growth will make it more difficult for Saskatchewan to achieve its end goals in GHG reduction. Saskatchewan will share many of the same challenges as its fossil fuel producing, resource-rich neighbour, Alberta.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">It is expected that Saskatchewan will stay true to its legislative and policy agenda of relying on technological innovation to lead emission reductions in the province rather than reducing <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;47" nep=";;49" hyphenated="n" folio="7-47" backFolio="7-48" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="3" releaseDate="2022;10;30" ?>demand through carbon pricing. This approach has been criticized as falling &ldquo;well short of what is required for Saskatchewan to do its fair share in reducing greenhouse gas emissions and helping Canada meet its commitments under the Paris Climate Accord&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">2nd Sess., 26th Leg., Saskatchewan, 2009 at p. 1.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
                </footnote>
              </para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
        <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
          <metadata.block metadata.store="yes">
            <metadata field="classification">
              <value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value>
            </metadata>
          </metadata.block>
          <section.front date.updated="0">
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
              <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
              <designator date.updated="0">7:48</designator>
              <name date.updated="0">Regional and International Linkages</name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body date.updated="0">
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">Saskatchewan&apos;s opposition to carbon pricing drives its linkages with other jurisdictions. Saskatchewan is the only province not to sign the 2016 Pan-Canadian Framework on Clean Growth and Climate Change, known as the Vancouver Declaration. Saskatchewan has also led litigation efforts to oppose the imposition of a carbon tax in the provinces that fail to price carbon themselves. In 2018, the province asked the Saskatchewan Court of Appeal to rule on the constitutionality of the <ital>Greenhouse Gas Pollution Pricing Act.</ital> When the court ruled that the federal government had the authority to impose the tax, Saskatchewan appealed.<footnote date.updated="0">
                  <footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference>
                  <footnote.body date.updated="0">
                    <para date.updated="0">
                      <para.text date.updated="0">
                        <cite finder="manual" type="case">
                          <ref p1="Reference re Greenhouse Gas Pollution Pricing Act" post.rptr="40" pre.rptr="2019" ref.id="I674a580049f611eb9940c10d1ac8a66e" title="Reference re Greenhouse Gas Pollution Pricing Act">Reference re Greenhouse Gas Pollution Pricing Act, 2019 SKCA 40 (Sask. C.A.)</ref>
                      </cite>.</para.text>
                    </para>
                  </footnote.body>
              </footnote> A future Supreme Court of Canada decision on this matter will likely shape how Saskatchewan engages regionally and internationally on climate change issues.</para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
      </section.block>
    </analytical.level.body>
  </analytical.level>
  <analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
    <front date.updated="0">
      <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
        <designator date.updated="0">VII</designator>
        <name date.updated="0">Conclusion</name>
      </outline.name.block>
    </front>
    <analytical.level.body date.updated="0">
      <section.block>
        <section date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
          <section.front date.updated="0">
            <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
              <label date.updated="0">&sect;</label>
              <designator date.updated="0">7:49</designator>
              <name date.updated="0">Concluding Remarks</name>
            </outline.name.block>
          </section.front>
          <section.body date.updated="0">
            <para date.updated="0">
              <para.text date.updated="0">Apart from the Arctic and the North, Saskatchewan stands to see some of Canada&apos;s most significant climate change impacts. Higher temperatures may mean longer growing seasons, but any positive impacts may be outweighed by increased weather variability and scarcity of water. On the other hand, Saskatchewan&apos;s &ldquo;new economy&rdquo; is largely resource based and is GHG intensive.</para.text>
            </para>
            <para date.updated="20221004">
              <para.text date.updated="0">In 2007, the Government of Saskatchewan announced a climate change plan that called for 32&percnt; per capita GHG emission reductions by 2020. The plan contemplated a broad range of initiatives in electricity conservation, emission reductions in the government sector, carbon sequestration and technology development. The change in government in 2008 brought with it a change in approach to climate change policy, but a stated commitment to achieve the same emission reduction targets. By February of 2009, the stated objective was dropped. It has been replaced by a &ldquo;Made in Saskatchewan&rdquo; plan. <ital>Prairie Resilience</ital> rejects climate pricing and contains what can only be described as unambitious targets to reduce GHGs.</para.text>
            </para>
          </section.body>
        </section>
      </section.block>
    </analytical.level.body>
  </analytical.level>
</chapter.body>
<chapter.back date.updated="0">
  <appendix.block date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
    <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart releaseDate="2022;10;30" sp=";;49" folio="7-49" releaseNumber="3" hyphenated="n" nep="5001" backBlank="y"?>
    <appendix online.doc.break="appendix" date.revised="0" date.updated="0">
      <appendix.front date.updated="0">
        <outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
          <label date.updated="0">Appendix</label>
          <designator date.updated="0">7A</designator>
          <name date.updated="0">Case Digest</name>
        </outline.name.block>
      </appendix.front>
      <appendix.body date.updated="0">
        <head align="l" toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
          <name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0">
            <name date.updated="0">
              <bold>Natural resources&mdash;Oil and gas&mdash;Practice and procedure&mdash;Evidence</bold>
            </name>
          </name.block>
        </head>
        <para date.updated="0">
          <para.text date.updated="0">Respondent operated oil pipeline that leaked and caused significant quantity of oil to spill into river&mdash;Environment and Climate Change Canada (ECCC) began investigation partially to determine whether respondent committed offences under <ital>Fisheries Act</ital>&mdash;Respondent claimed litigation privilege over 96 documents, and solicitor-client privilege over 9 documents&mdash;Crown brought application for release of documents&mdash;Application granted in part&mdash;Litigation was reasonably apprehended by respondent soon after oil spill was discovered&mdash;Respondent created investigation charter which guided its internal investigation and was expected from outset to remain confidential&mdash;While respondent was required to provide report regarding spill to authorities under statutory and regulatory obligations, dominant purpose of investigation charter was to prepare for litigation&mdash;Respondent did not waive privilege&mdash;All but three documents were subject to privilege: <ital>Her Majesty The Queen v. Husky Energy Inc.</ital> (2017), 2017 CarswellSask 695, 2017 SKQB 383, J.D. Kalmakoff J. (Sask. Q.B.) &lsqb;Saskatchewan&rsqb;</para.text>
        </para>
      </appendix.body>
    </appendix>
  </appendix.block>
</chapter.back>
</chapter>